our basic Conspiracy is roughly 20 people willing to go reasonably outside the law in order to fulfill their goals. Those 20 people are <i>not</i> going to collapse a government on their own, regardless of how good they are. See the table below for the successes necessary to overreach or extend your grasp.<br /> <br /> A Cult takes Actions just like a character might. They may perform only one Instant Action (rolled or unrolled) at their full level of either Reach, Grasp, or Lore. They may perform two Reflexive Actions at Reach/Grasp/Lore -1, and an unlimited amount of Reflexive Actions at Reach/Grasp/Lore -2. While they are termed Reflexive or Instant Actions, the Cult\u2019s work occurs over a period of time, generally one day per dot of Reach, Grasp, or Lore.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\"><b>Reach or Grasp Exceeded</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\"><b>Successes Needed</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">1 dot</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">1 Success</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">2 dots</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">3 Successes</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">3 dots</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">9 Successes</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">4 dots</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">12 Successes</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">5 dots</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"padding: 0px\">25 Successes</td> </tr> </table></div> <b>Roll Results</b><ul><li><b>Dramatic Failure: </b>Inflict any number of debilitating Conditions listed below in the Appendix.</li> <li><b>Failure:</b> Inflict the Flagged or Contender Condition</li> <li><b>Success:</b> Yay! It worked!</li> <li><b>Exceptional Success:</b> Gain the Inspired or Informed Condition</li> </ul><br /> <b>Blocked: </b>Organizations undercut and work against each other all the time. This can be either a casual thing, or serious acts of sabotage. A Cult may dedicate a number of dots of Reach, Grasp, or Lore to oppose the actions of a rival Cult. Dots dedicated in this way reduce their opponent\u2019s traits by the same amount, but also reduces the effectiveness of your own Cult. You could act as a minor annoyance to another organization, or dedicate time and resources into seriously impeding their efforts. Just remember that action tends to encourage reprisal. If you dedicate a number of dots equal to the <i>lowest</i> of Reach, Grasp, or Lore, your Cult is considered to have taken an Instant Action as appropriate. (See <b>When Cults Roll</b> above) Dots dedicated in this way are temporarily suppressed for a number of days equal to the dots dedicated. Conspirators can be dedicated to this action as well.<br /> <br /> <b>Social Maneuvering with Cults:</b> This isn\u2019t so much trying to get someone to do what you want as it is performing acts of diplomacy. Declare a Goal and decide Initial Impression as normal. A Cult possesses doors equal to the lower of their Reach or Grasp. If the action would be considered a Breaking Point, add two Doors. The Cult\u2019s Virtue, Vice, and Aspirations should be based on their <i>Doctrine</i>, <i>Foundation,</i> and <i>Purpose</i> respectively. Going against or appealing to these traits adds and removes Doors normally. When rolling, use Reach, Grasp, or Lore depending on what\u2019s appropriate. Factor in the benefits of the Leverage and Conspirator Merits. (Note: A Cult can also engage in Social Maneuvering with a single character, but that generally entails the Cult Leader or a Conspirator approaching them and rolling normally. Benefits from Leverage and multiple Conspirators in one organization apply normally.)<br /> <br /> Other than that, Social Maneuvering functions as written.<br /> <br /> <b>Holy Wars: </b>Cults can always just go to war directly, miniature crusades in back alleys, bombs in mailboxes and Molotovs through storefronts and safe-house windows. In such a case, roll Grasp vs. Grasp as an Instant Action, as combat is always a fickle and unpredictable situation. Much like Down and Dirty Combat, state an Intent going in- that you want to destroy weapons stockpiles, for example, or assassinate a particular Conspirator. If successful, the result depends on your dots of Grasp. If you have <i>lower </i>Grasp than your opponent, nothing serious happens, and the reprisal is likely to be swift. If you have <i>higher</i> Grasp, then you destroy a Merit dot per dot of Grasp that exceeds that of your opponent.<br /> <br /> Such an action could outright destroy Reach and Grasp through killing Cult members, destroy Lore by killing the right people, defacing altars, burning books, destroy Benefits by shaking the reputation and mindset of the enemy Cult (Mystery Cult Initiation is immune to this), and stranger things besides. An assault upon a powerful gang could lead to a loss of Home Turf as people recognize they don\u2019t totally control the streets. Conspirators die, Leverage is lost, and Legitimacy could collapse under a \u2018False Flag\u2019 operation or a frame up to discredit the enemy Cult. When Cults go to war, nothing is truly holy.<br /> <br /> <b>Investigating Cults:</b> The number of Clues you need to Uncover the Truth about a given Cult is equal to their dots of Grasp.<br /> <br /> <b>Building Equipment: </b>Cults can use the Building Equipment rules, substituting Reach, Grasp, and Lore for regular dice pools where appropriate. The equipment thus established can benefit the whole Cult, but generally goes away if it is targeted in an attack (see <b>Holy Wars</b> above. Each piece of equipment counts for one point).</div> </div> </div> </div>You know thats silly of me I didn't think to use Legitimacy, Leverage, or Conspirators alongside isolated. That a very good point. But great work so far.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T07:51:03-07:00","id":858997,"title":"","content":"Frankly, I think those three Merits can be infinitely more useful than an outright bigger Cult. Especially since it can make the Cult less of an Experience sinkhole. That being said, Mummies will tend to blow <i>a lot </i>of Experiences on Cults, which is exactly as it should be. Conspirators are a lot more vulnerable than the Cult itself, but they are <i>extremely </i>useful, acting as a sort of combination Ally/Retainer/Contact. This continues to branch along the lines of a Cult being an- albeit rather specialized, upkeep heavy, and unstable -&quot;omni-Social&quot; Merit.<br /> <br /> And now you have everything I've written in the last little while. Fortunately, your feedback is giving me <i>a lot </i>more to work with. If you have ideas for different Foundations, new ways to use and/or manipulate Cults, or things to build off Cults, don't hesitate to make suggestions. That being said, I <i>am </i>still intending on writing a crossover section that describes the utility of Cult in various games, including how to judge how powerful a given Werewolf Pack is based on how many dots are dedicated into Totem and/or how many Wolf-Blooded there are.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Battle Fodder: </b>There is a final common, albeit unwise, use for Cults. Their master may call upon them to pick up weapons and fight by his side. The trappings vary based on Foundation. An Enterprise or Elite Club might hire bodyguards and private security, Gangs and Cult of Personality send in wild-eyed youths wearing symbols and colors, Conspiracies favor inconspicuous infiltrators and gunmen. A group of Tribal combatants goes into battle wearing holy symbols and chanting hymns, while an Unorganized Cult charges in, ragged and varied anarchists hell-bent on destruction. These fighters aren\u2019t necessarily competent, but they\u2019re keen. Their numbers depend on how long the Cult takes to muster its forces. Remember to factor in the Wrathful Benefit if you have it.<ul><li><i>Under 24 Hours</i><b>:</b> Roll Reach + Grasp. You get a combatant per success.</li> <li><i>24 Hours</i><b>:</b> You get a number of minions equal to the higher of Reach or Grasp</li> <li><i>48 Hours</i><b>:</b> You get an armed fighter for each dot of Reach and Grasp</li> </ul>There is a drawback to this though. If you have any kind of Cult except for a Gang, and someone dies in the fighting, you lose a point of Reach or Grasp (whichever is higher). Death tends to dissuade people from working for you. If more than ten people die in a single battle, you lose an additional dot of Reach or Grasp.<br /> <br /> <i>Fighting for Advanced Cults</i><b>:</b> For every dot of Advanced Reach or Advanced Grasp, you get ten combatants for every success or dot. This does stack, so an Advanced Grasp Conspiracy with three dots in the dedicated Merit would gain <i>30</i> combatants per dot and/or success.<br /> <br /> Cult combatants use the following stats:<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Combatant Type</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Best At</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Worst At</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Everything Else</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Willpower/Scene</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Minion</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Chance</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Militant</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Militarized</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> </tr> </table></div> <ul><li><b>Health: </b>7</li> <li><b>Defense =</b> Everything Else</li> <li><b>Speed = </b>5 + Everything Else</li> <li><b>Initiative = </b>Best At</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T08:05:43-07:00","id":859650,"title":"","content":"Alright, revised Lore and new stuff regarding Agents and Operatives, which are really just the elder siblings of Conspirators. Honestly, something that's really coming out as I continue to develop this is that, as satisfying as it is to have a massive Cult all to yourself, a massive Cult with lots of personal Reach and Grasp just <i>isn't</i> all that Experience efficient. With these new sets of rules, establishing far-reaching, many-tentacled organizations through Conspirators as they evolve into Agents and Operatives isn't just more thematic, but more effective as well.<br /> <br /> That said, you can establish a <i>ton </i>of different kinds of Cults. This play style, relatively small but with far-reaching and influential Agents and Operatives, is more suited to a Conspiracy, Enterprise, or Elite Club than anything else. Another equally valid play style is a Cult that is all-powerful in their own territory, but not all that influential elsewhere... which leads back to Conspirators again. Conspirators and its companion Merits are going to be an excellent way to build up a Cult and single out a couple signature characters within it. I intend to elaborate more on that in the crossover section, &quot;Building the Pyramid&quot;.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Lore (o - ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Cult 1+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Lore is, effectively, a measure of the Occult Skill spread across the entire Cult- how aware of the supernatural they all are. Members of the Cult can be guaranteed to have as many dots in Occult as they have dots in Initiation or Status equal to (5-Lore). So, a not terribly knowledgeable Cult is grasping at straws (Werewolves don\u2019t like silver, right?) While a <i>very </i>knowledgeable one knows the ins and outs of the supernatural community; the average Cult member has some idea, while the High Priests are experts. (No! I am <i>not</i> just going to talk to a faerie/demon/spirit- not without a lawyer present.) Bigger Cults tend to have less Lore, if only because it\u2019s harder to distribute amongst the members, and- more importantly -because it\u2019s safer for the supernatural being at the heart of the Cult.<br /> <br /> <b>Second Sight (o)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 3+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Cult members are more likely to possess Supernatural Merits, whether by virtue of their studies, some part of the Initiation practice, or simple exposure to weirdness. A Cult that deals heavily with the Shadow will likely have a few Mediums and/or people exhibiting Tells, for example, while a Cult that interacts with Vampires may possess Clear-Sighted so that concealing Disciplines cannot fool them. These Supernatural Merits are independent of any gained through a Mystery Cult Initiation tethered to the same Cult. The Cultists gain access to a single Merit worth (Lore-2, minimum 1) Experiences, and it kicks in when the occult knowledge of Lore would. This Merit may be purchased multiple times to represent multiple gifts from joining the Cult. While generally only Supernatural Merits apply, something like Iron Will would be suitable as well.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Unless this Merit is paired with the Blas\u00e9 Benefit above, or the Merit is something like Sleepwalker or a Wolfblooded Tell, then for every instance of this Merit the Cultists take a -1 penalty to Integrity rolls due to supernatural causes. The forbidden lore is starting to claw a bit at their mind.<br /> <br /> <b>Theurgy (o)<br /> Prerequisite: </b>Second Sight<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Cultists are possessed of more than some paltry gifts, and are instead more comparable to relatively competent mediums or hedge witches. Double the Merit dots granted by Second Sight; this can grant a wider range of abilities to the Cultists, adding some variation to the group, and it can further build upon a theme.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Cultists with Supernatural Merits gain a suitable Persistent Condition, something like Bewitched or Receptive. It can be both a blessing, and a curse, as it makes using their supernatural abilities easier, but not necessarily safer. Just as with Second Sight, this Merit may be taken multiple times and tagged to a separate Second Sight Merit (or perhaps the same one), in which case use only the Conditions that apply based on the selected Merits.<br /> <br /> <b>Thaumaturgy (o)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Theurgy<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Double the Merit dots again, granting a total of twelve maximum. The Cultists possess a wide range of abilities, ranging from the small and paltry to the vast and powerful, but one thing that is certain is that they are skilled and potentially dangerous practitioners of various forms of magic.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Cultists with this much magical power find that it puts quite a bit of strain on their souls. Their Integrity is capped at 9. As with Theurgy and Second Sight, this Merit may be purchased multiple times, reducing maximum Integrity on Cultists benefitting from it with each purchase.<br /> <br /> <b>Patron (o)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite:</b> Second Sight<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Your Cult serves, worships, or is otherwise involved with a particular kind of supernatural being, giving them access to abilities they normally would not possess. In addition to Supernatural Merits, the Cult may access Dread Powers, Rites, Disciplines, Rotes, Contracts, Affinities, and other such abilities with Storyteller discretion.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> Your Cult is specifically bound to a supernatural being, and is expected to do their bidding. Take a suitable Persistent Condition that applies to <i>all</i> Cultists, and add take a Short Term Aspiration related to the commands of the Patron.<br /> <br /> <b>Endowment (ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 1+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>You Cult comes with an Endowment. Design one that is thematically appropriate. The Cult possessing the Endowment Merit does <i>not</i> mean that Cultists will automatically possess it without the Second Sight &gt; Theurgy &gt; Thaumaturgy Merit chain.<br /> <br /> <b>Agent (o - ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Conspirator<br /> <b>Effect: </b>As it stands, a Conspirator functions like a two-dot Ally/Retainer, without counting the boost to Reach and Grasp they grant. By investing more heavily in one particular Conspirator, the Cult can make them an Agent- a formidable asset more than capable of acting alone. First, add dots in Agent to Conspirator to determine what level of Retainer they are. Secondly, distribute dots of Agent between Reach and Grasp to illustrate the Agent\u2019s <i>personal</i> level of influence. By spending a point of Willpower, a Cult Leader may have an Agent act alone for a Chapter the way they would use the base Cult\u2019s Reach or Grasp, with one major exception. The Agent can still act as a Retainer would, performing an <i>individual</i> rather than an <i>institutional</i> task.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> An Agent who acts alone can get themselves killed on a mission gone badly, and in acting alone they also get tagged with Conditions <i>specifically</i> to themselves, and not to their parent Cult. This is something of a mixed blessing, putting the Agent at <i>much</i> higher risk than before, while also providing the Cult with a bit more temporary security.<br /> <br /> <b>Operative (o - ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Agent at equal or higher value<br /> <b>Effect: </b>For every dot of the parent Cult\u2019s Reach and Grasp (<i>not</i> the Agent\u2019s personal Reach and Grasp) the Operative gains a number of additional Merit dots equal to their dots in Operative. This is purely to benefit the Operative, representing their widespread influence and personal resources.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>An Operative is far, far more independent than an Agent or a Conspirator, and no longer acts as a Retainer, but as an Ally. While you can still spend a point of Willpower to get an Operative to act on their own for the Chapter, you\u2019ll need to roll Manipulation + Persuasion - (Reach or Grasp over what the Operative possesses) they\u2019re already putting a very prosperous life on the line just working for you, and they\u2019re starting to resent working for someone. Whether the roll fails or succeeds, you will not be able to use the Operative next chapter. On a Dramatic Failure, the Operative goes rogue and seeks retribution, giving you the Rogue Cult condition.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T09:40:10-07:00","id":859701,"title":"","content":"Very nice. A lot more developed and flavorful than what is currently there (or my half-written Organizations As Characters notes). I look forward to seeing what else comes of this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T14:13:32-07:00","id":859862,"title":"","content":"Thanks. I'm having a blast writing it so far. Next thing coming up, if I don't think of some other Merits that build off of Cult, Lore, or other such things, will be &quot;Building the Pyramid&quot; the Storyteller/Player/Crossover Guide to Cults. Fun fun fun. Complete with advice on how to turn Conspirators/Agents/Operatives into important, fleshed-out characters in a Cult, or even <i>playing </i>as a Conspirator/Agent/Operative, which would be a heck of a lot of fun. I expect to get the most mileage out of Demon, Mummy, and Mage for that section.<br /> <br /> Oh, and something I've been wanting to do with this Cult system ever since I started writing it. Adapt it to Dark Eras so that you can build/lead a tribe/village etc. Would be extremely fun in one of the less defined settings, like the Neolithic or First Turn Kemet.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Altar (ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 1+, Safe Place 1+<br /> <b>Effect:</b> A Cult with such an artifact, either found, made by hand, or otherwise acquired, may use the Teamwork rules to use Supernatural Merits or other powers of the Cult, even if they don\u2019t possess any dots in the Merit, or the necessary metaphysical prerequisites to wield the magic in the first place. The Extended Action to use this Altar is generally 30 minutes per roll, but this doubles if supporting actors don\u2019t have all the necessary traits.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> You need <i>time</i> to gather the cultists necessary to perform a ritual together, and an Altar generally isn\u2019t <i>mobile</i>. The time to gather ritualists is roughly the same as the time it takes to gather combatants.<br /> <br /> <b>Dedicated Altar (ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Altar, Safe Place 2+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The mystical symbolism embedded in the Cult\u2019s Altar is much, much more potent than is was before, even serving as a kind of reliquary for the mystical energies of a ritual. Cultists may worship before the Dedicated Altar in an Extended Presence + Expression roll lead by the Cult Leader, with all attendant cultists acting as supporting actors. Each success on the roll generates a point of the Fuel Stat used by the Cult Leader and/or their Patron, and can hold a number of points equal to three times the Merit dots spent on the Dedicated Altar. So, Willpower/Essence/Aether/Vitae/Pillars/Aether etc. These points may be spent on anything they would normally use the Altar for, and drain away at the rate of a point every (Lore) days.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>You still need to gather up ritualists, and more importantly, now the Altar smells <i>delicious</i> to the appropriate supernatural beings, pinging on their senses. Cultists just might have to fight to avoid sharing their Altar with an unwanted Spirit or prevent it from being co-opted by an industrious Angel.<br /> <br /> <b>Consecrated Altar (ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Dedicated Altar, Patron, Safe Place 3+<br /> <b>Effect:</b> The Altar now permanently possesses a Base Condition that could, theoretically, produce the necessary supernatural fuel for the rites. This means that if it was alluring before, now it is irresistibly desirable. A Consecrated Altar for a God-Machine Cult is Infrastructure, while a carved standing stone becomes Resonant thanks to the attention of a group of Spirit worshippers. A Consecrated Altar could be incorporated into, or form the base of, something like a Locus, Tomb/Temple, Haunt, or Subverted Infrastructure.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>The trouble that could come from the Condition now tied to the Consecrated Altar is drawback enough. Have fun defending your place of worship.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Appendix</u></b><b>- Conditions</b><br /> <br /> <b>Admirer (Persistent): </b>Someone has latched onto your Cult, or you personally, and is expressing nothing but the utmost admiration for your capabilities and what you can do. In other words, an obsessive stalker who\u2019s going to cause you trouble or who is trouble.<br /> <b>Beat: </b>Your admirer causes you, or your Cult, a significant problem<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>The admirer dies, joins your Cult, or somehow loses track of you<br /> <br /> <b>Civil War (Persistent): </b>Something has pushed your Cult to the tipping point, and now relatively normal tension or conflict has erupted into an outright war. You cannot use your Cult for anything so long as this Condition persists. Upon gaining this Condition, or at the beginning of the next session if it persists for more than one, roll Reach vs. Grasp following the sidebar <b>Holy War</b> above. If the sides are equal, or if one is higher than the other, then the other trait loses a number of dots equal to the difference (minimum 1)<br /> <b>Possible Source:</b> Dramatic Failure on a Cult roll, Failure with the Contender Condition<br /> <b>Beat: </b>You are severely inconvenienced by your lack of a cult<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>Do something drastic to bring the Cult to heel- this usually imposes a Breaking Point on the Cult Leader.<br /> <br /> <b>Contender: </b>Someone in the Cult is waiting in the wings to rise up in the ranks, and doesn\u2019t mind stepping over their fellow Cultists in order to do so. Your next Failure with a Cult action is considered a Dramatic Failure- upgrade this Condition to the Civil War or Rogue Cult Condition as appropriate.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> Find the upstart and take them out. Alternatively, give them what they want. Fail a roll and have this Condition upgraded to Civil War or Rogue Cult.<br /> <br /> <b>Flagged: </b>Someone is suspicious of you. It could be the police or social services investigating what they think is a mundane problem, or Hunters investigating what they\u2019re sure is a supernatural problem. Just remember, even a parking ticket can become a problem if a police officer finds evidence of human sacrifice. Mechanically, another Cult starts an Investigation action against yours.<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>The Investigation concludes, upgrading the Condition to Hunted. The Investigation concludes, and they fail to find anything of value.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunted (Persistent): </b>The jig is up and someone is out to arrest, or more likely, kill you.<br /> <b>Beat: </b>Your pursuers cause you a significant problem<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>Convince the Hunters to leave you be, give them what they want, kill them, or otherwise lose them for good.<br /> <br /> <b>Record (Persistent):</b> You\u2019ve dealt with the immediate fallout of a newsworthy event, or moved into the public eye, and now there\u2019s a paper trail. Any attempt to investigate the Cult (not you personally) benefit, gaining an additional Clue on a successful Investigation action per instance of the Record Condition.<br /> <b>Beat: </b>An investigation succeeds or comes dangerously close because of the Record<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>Become big enough to lean on the organization that previously tracked you, completely reorganize the Cult so that it has cut ties with its past.<br /> <br /> <b>Rogue Cult (Persistent): </b>An organization has cropped up without your consent or knowledge and is causing trouble- affiliating themselves with you or your own Cult. Alternatively, part of your Cult has broken free and now seeks to oppose you. A Rogue Cult possesses a Foundation and all other basic parts of a Cult, and except in instances when a Cult breaks off from your own organization (outlined above) the Rogue Cult gains a number of dots to distribute amongst the various Cult Merits equal to the lower of your Reach or Grasp.<br /> <b>Beat: </b>The Cult causes a significant problem for you or your original organization<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>The Rogue Cult is brought under control (requires either an Extended Action or a Breaking Point), the Rogue Cult is destroyed or disbanded.<br /> <br /> <b>Wronged (Persistent):</b> You have offended someone personally, and they are bringing their not inconsiderable resources to bear against your organization. If they ever learn of the Cult\u2019s supernatural nature (if it has one) the person is rather liable to snap and become a Hunter. Mechanically, treat them as a Retainer with the lower of Reach or Grasp as dots dedicated to the Merit.<br /> <b>Beat:</b> The person pursuing you cause a significant problem<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> They die, get to confront you (even if it\u2019s just to give you a piece of their mind), or are inducted into the Cult (requires an Extended Action with successes equal to their effective dots of Retainer x 5)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T14:28:37-07:00","id":859877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859862#post859862\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Altar (ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 1+, Safe Place 1+<br /> <b>Effect:</b> A Cult with such an artifact, either found, made by hand, or otherwise acquired, may use the Teamwork rules to use Supernatural Merits or other powers of the Cult, even if they don\u2019t possess any dots in the Merit, or the necessary metaphysical prerequisites to wield the magic in the first place. The Extended Action to use this Altar is generally 30 minutes per roll, but this doubles if supporting actors don\u2019t have all the necessary traits.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> You need <i>time</i> to gather the cultists necessary to perform a ritual together, and an Altar generally isn\u2019t <i>mobile</i>. The time to gather ritualists is roughly the same as the time it takes to gather combatants.</div> </div> </div> </div>How does this work with something like, say, telekinesis or psychokinesis? Or is this a case where, while you can invoke those Supernatural Merits, why would you bother?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T16:00:28-07:00","id":859946,"title":"","content":"You've got it. It's a case of &quot;Why bother?&quot; Yeah, sure, you could unleash a ludicrously fantastic light show, but what would be the point of that? Altars are really more useful for buffing powers that have a timer, powers that hang, function at a distance, could <i>really </i>benefit from a lot of Teamwork dice, or is otherwise unaccessible to the cultists. Of course, there are edge cases where there are mobile Altars which could make using Telekinesis or Psychokinesis in their presence far, far more useful. Something like, oh, say, the Ark of the Covenant.<br /> <br /> But yeah, Altars are more for long drawn out rituals more than quick-and-dirty magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T17:20:25-07:00","id":859985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Lore (o - ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Cult 1+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Lore is, effectively, a measure of the Occult Skill spread across the entire Cult- how aware of the supernatural they all are. Members of the Cult can be guaranteed to have as many dots in Occult as they have dots in Initiation or Status equal to (5-Lore). So, a not terribly knowledgeable Cult is grasping at straws (Werewolves don\u2019t like silver, right?) While a <i>very </i>knowledgeable one knows the ins and outs of the supernatural community; the average Cult member has some idea, while the High Priests are experts. (No! I am <i>not</i> just going to talk to a faerie/demon/spirit- not without a lawyer present.) Bigger Cults tend to have less Lore, if only because it\u2019s harder to distribute amongst the members, and- more importantly -because it\u2019s safer for the supernatural being at the heart of the Cult.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is great in ideal, but I can't help but feels that they should have a Lesser understanding of the supernal world, after all most splats are very good at hiding themselves. Also humans can only gather so much on their own. Maybe have them know one for sure truth about the world and gain the knowledge of one supernatural group in the world per dot of lore.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Second Sight (o)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 3+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Cult members are more likely to possess Supernatural Merits, whether by virtue of their studies, some part of the Initiation practice, or simple exposure to weirdness. A Cult that deals heavily with the Shadow will likely have a few Mediums and/or people exhibiting Tells, for example, while a Cult that interacts with Vampires may possess Clear-Sighted so that concealing Disciplines cannot fool them. These Supernatural Merits are independent of any gained through a Mystery Cult Initiation tethered to the same Cult. The Cultists gain access to a single Merit worth (Lore-2, minimum 1) Experiences, and it kicks in when the occult knowledge of Lore would. This Merit may be purchased multiple times to represent multiple gifts from joining the Cult. While generally only Supernatural Merits apply, something like Iron Will would be suitable as well.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Unless this Merit is paired with the Blas\u00e9 Benefit above, or the Merit is something like Sleepwalker or a Wolfblooded Tell, then for every instance of this Merit the Cultists take a -1 penalty to Integrity rolls due to supernatural causes. The forbidden lore is starting to claw a bit at their mind.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Theurgy (o)<br /> Prerequisite: </b>Second Sight<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Cultists are possessed of more than some paltry gifts, and are instead more comparable to relatively competent mediums or hedge witches. Double the Merit dots granted by Second Sight; this can grant a wider range of abilities to the Cultists, adding some variation to the group, and it can further build upon a theme.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Cultists with Supernatural Merits gain a suitable Persistent Condition, something like Bewitched or Receptive. It can be both a blessing, and a curse, as it makes using their supernatural abilities easier, but not necessarily safer. Just as with Second Sight, this Merit may be taken multiple times and tagged to a separate Second Sight Merit (or perhaps the same one), in which case use only the Conditions that apply based on the selected Merits.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Thaumaturgy (o)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Theurgy<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Double the Merit dots again, granting a total of twelve maximum. The Cultists possess a wide range of abilities, ranging from the small and paltry to the vast and powerful, but one thing that is certain is that they are skilled and potentially dangerous practitioners of various forms of magic.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Cultists with this much magical power find that it puts quite a bit of strain on their souls. Their Integrity is capped at 9. As with Theurgy and Second Sight, this Merit may be purchased multiple times, reducing maximum Integrity on Cultists benefitting from it with each purchase.</div> </div> </div> </div>These is a great merit line but shouldn't they cost more then 1 Dot each? Think about it that like 12 supernatural merits dots by spend 8. Also does every member have access to these magical abilities? Or is there a special wing of the cult that has the magic like how certain members are recruited for fighting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Patron (o)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite:</b> Second Sight<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Your Cult serves, worships, or is otherwise involved with a particular kind of supernatural being, giving them access to abilities they normally would not possess. In addition to Supernatural Merits, the Cult may access Dread Powers, Rites, Disciplines, Rotes, Contracts, Affinities, and other such abilities with Storyteller discretion.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> Your Cult is specifically bound to a supernatural being, and is expected to do their bidding. Take a suitable Persistent Condition that applies to <i>all</i> Cultists, and add take a Short Term Aspiration related to the commands of the Patron.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Endowment (ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 1+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>You Cult comes with an Endowment. Design one that is thematically appropriate. The Cult possessing the Endowment Merit does <i>not</i> mean that Cultists will automatically possess it without the Second Sight &gt; Theurgy &gt; Thaumaturgy Merit chain.</div> </div> </div> </div>These I don't like to much, the idea of just getting access to a supernaturals Spells feel wrong to me especially since it so cheap. There a plethora of reasons why this can be such as Supernal magic needs the Supernal and only mummies can use Utterances.<br /> As for endowment it that feels like all that should stay a Hunter thing. It's one of the few thing that lets them standout.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Agent (o - ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Conspirator<br /> <b>Effect: </b>As it stands, a Conspirator functions like a two-dot Ally/Retainer, without counting the boost to Reach and Grasp they grant. By investing more heavily in one particular Conspirator, the Cult can make them an Agent- a formidable asset more than capable of acting alone. First, add dots in Agent to Conspirator to determine what level of Retainer they are. Secondly, distribute dots of Agent between Reach and Grasp to illustrate the Agent\u2019s <i>personal</i> level of influence. By spending a point of Willpower, a Cult Leader may have an Agent act alone for a Chapter the way they would use the base Cult\u2019s Reach or Grasp, with one major exception. The Agent can still act as a Retainer would, performing an <i>individual</i> rather than an <i>institutional</i> task.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> An Agent who acts alone can get themselves killed on a mission gone badly, and in acting alone they also get tagged with Conditions <i>specifically</i> to themselves, and not to their parent Cult. This is something of a mixed blessing, putting the Agent at <i>much</i> higher risk than before, while also providing the Cult with a bit more temporary security.<br /> <br /> <b>Operative (o - ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Agent at equal or higher value<br /> <b>Effect: </b>For every dot of the parent Cult\u2019s Reach and Grasp (<i>not</i> the Agent\u2019s personal Reach and Grasp) the Operative gains a number of additional Merit dots equal to their dots in Operative. This is purely to benefit the Operative, representing their widespread influence and personal resources.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>An Operative is far, far more independent than an Agent or a Conspirator, and no longer acts as a Retainer, but as an Ally. While you can still spend a point of Willpower to get an Operative to act on their own for the Chapter, you\u2019ll need to roll Manipulation + Persuasion - (Reach or Grasp over what the Operative possesses) they\u2019re already putting a very prosperous life on the line just working for you, and they\u2019re starting to resent working for someone. Whether the roll fails or succeeds, you will not be able to use the Operative next chapter. On a Dramatic Failure, the Operative goes rogue and seeks retribution, giving you the Rogue Cult condition.</div> </div> </div> </div>I fucking love the application and use of these merits it not only makes your cult feel smaller in scope but it gives you characters to flush out and showing just how Cults Should get the power manipulation of other factions.<br /> <br /> But fear not, I hate to reject thing without giving an alternatives in turn.<br /> if I might recommend this or something similar to replace the magical benefits of the second sight line.<br /> <b> Coven (o-ooooo) </b><br /> <b> Prerequisite: </b> Lore equal or higher value.<br /> <b>Effect: </b> Your cult has a cabal of spellcaster dedicated to your cause. For every dot of Coven you have the cabal of spellcaster gain a dot Gnosis (HV:WF). You have a base amount of spellcasters equal to 3+coven+lore. You can recruit more spellcasters with extended roll Presences+Occult with a number of success needed equal to 5 + 1 for every spellcaster over the base amount. The cabal has access to every spells of their chosen Mystery so long as the spell level is equal to or lower then your dots in Cabal. The cabals dice pool for all spells is Lore+Cabal dots. Lastly spell cast from the cabal has a greater range, the cabal has no upper when targeting fellow cultists and automaticly has a sympathetic link to them.<br /> <b> Drawback: </b> the power of sorcery comes at a price. First the cabal can only cast spells as rituals spells, as such they will always need ingredients. You cult can acquire necessary ingredients with a combination of your cults Grasp+Reach+1. <br /> Other Cults can intervene with your Cults cabal, by blocking your attempts of gaining ingredients in the same way as they would block your Grasp or Reach. Further more they can target &amp; attack members of your cabal. If enemy cults kill enough of your spellcaster that you no longer have the minimum amount needed for casting a spell then casting that spell becomes impossible until you recruit more spellcaster.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":10820,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","user":"Blakemikizuki","date":".  Needed to put dice pool 04-05-2016, 08:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T19:28:56-07:00","id":860054,"title":"","content":"I think I'm most proud of the Conspirator &gt; Agent &gt; Operative progression; it's an organic one that allows you to cram a <i>whole </i>lot of character into one Merit chain, and encourages you to elaborate on the character as much as possible.<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that with Lore, the effective Occult dots of a Cultist is determined by their Status/Initiation, with Lore kicking in at (5-Lore) Status/Initiation. So even the most knowledgeable Cult can have only the most casual initiate comment something along the lines of &quot;Yes, salt <i>does</i> help repel Ephemeral Beings&quot; or &quot;Yes, the fae <i>are </i>harmed by Iron.&quot; It's the High Priests of a super knowledgeable Cult that have a really good grasp of the supernatural world, and they only make up 5% of the membership.<br /> <br /> Secondly, in defense of the Second Sight Merit chain, it's actually <i>less </i>Experience efficient than the currently existing Mystery Cult initiation and Professional Training. Mystery Cult Initiation gives you ten Merit dots for five, and Professional Training gives you six Experiences for five plus three major advantages not listed. Eight Merit dots for twelve is somewhere in between those two, and more importantly the Second Sight Merit chain comes with some <i>serious </i>kickbacks. Notably, a hit to your ability to handle Breaking Points, a Persistent Condition you cannot get rid of that is quite liable to inflict serious penalties at an inopportune time, and a cap to Integrity. I do agree that exactly how many members of the Cult have access to these abilities should be outlined, which is something I'm going to edit in, but I disagree that Patron is too much of a bonus. Unless a Cultist is also a Sleepwalker, ill-gotten Rotes are still going to damage their Integrity. Disciplines, which even freak out <i>vampires </i>at higher Humanity levels, should almost certainly do the same. Also worth noting that a Mummy's Cultists could possibly gain <i>Affinities</i>, not Utterances. Affinities being <i>much </i>lower key, and entirely plausible if the Cultist has some way of purchasing the Stolen Pillars Numen, and even then at best they'd have a Pillar 2 Soul Affinity. I imagine most mortals <i>never </i>get involved in the Guilds, and as a result never get the chance to manifest Guild Affinities (but I think they would, if they did).<br /> <br /> Lastly, the only reason Endowment is there is if you intend to model a Hunter Compact or Conspiracy with a Cult.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T20:55:07-07:00","id":860087,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's worth noting that with Lore, the effective Occult dots of a Cultist is determined by their Status/Initiation, with Lore kicking in at (5-Lore) Status/Initiation. So even the most knowledgeable Cult can have only the most casual initiate comment something along the lines of &quot;Yes, salt does help repel Ephemeral Beings&quot; or &quot;Yes, the fae are harmed by Iron.&quot; It's the High Priests of a super knowledgeable Cult that have a really good grasp of the supernatural world, and they only make up 5% of the membership.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll be honest for some reason I was thinking that knowledge was for literally everyone in the cult. I'm very glad I'm wrong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but I disagree that Patron is too much of a bonus. </div> </div> </div> </div>I understand a cult can only get one choice of a supernatural \u201cspell\u201d system. But still allowing that for only one dot is very cheap. Honestly I think I would prefer something similar to an Werewolf's pack totem, a ephemeral entire that has taken a shine to the cult and give benefit to every cultist. At a price of course.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unless a Cultist is also a Sleepwalker, ill-gotten Rotes are still going to damage their Integrity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well if the supernatural merits are taught then that would actually make them sleepwalkers. But if the powers are bestowed from trinkets or things like that then they would be sleepers.<br /> But a major thing about awakening is that only mages can use supernal magic. Allowing cultists to gain Rotes even at the price of their souls feels cheapening the lonely aspect of mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Disciplines, which even freak out vampires at higher Humanity levels, should almost certainly do the same. </div> </div> </div> </div> also good point, you could have it where after the scene in which they used supernatural powers they have to make a breaking point. That would definitely showcase the downsides.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also worth noting that a Mummy's Cultists could possibly gain Affinities, not Utterances. Affinities being much lower key, and entirely plausible if the Cultist has some way of purchasing the Stolen Pillars Numen, and even then at best they'd have a Pillar 2 Soul Affinity. I imagine most mortals never get involved in the Guilds, and as a result never get the chance to manifest Guild Affinities (but I think they would, if they did).</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm I thought affinity were also mummy only, very well carry on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=859650#post859650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Lastly, the only reason Endowment is there is if you intend to model a Hunter Compact or Conspiracy with a Cult.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a point I did not considered actually. <br /> But I think a Cult to represent an Compact or Conspiracy would be something thing smaller then the entire group, and most definitely larger then cell. Sort of like regional sects of the group. <br /> <br /> I mean Task Force Valkyrie for example cover all of NATO and backed by vampires it should be larger then what most secret society bring together in a few decades. And definitely harder to forever keep at bay then spending 6 exp.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T12:50:31-07:00","id":860491,"title":"","content":"Here's an answer for your question. And some basic guidelines to building Cults.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <i>How Many Empowered Cultists?</i> <b>-</b> Excellent question, and one that wasn\u2019t addressed above. In some Cults, minor supernatural gifts are going to be more common than in others, while for a few supernatural powers are reserved only for the elite. The gift given by Second Sight applies to <i>all</i> Cultists when Lore would kick in, so upon initiation at the highest levels, and at High Priesthood at the lowest. For a good yardstick based upon a Lore 5 Cult with Second Sight through to Thaumaturgy, see below.<br /> <b>o - </b>Benefits of the Second Sight Merit (3 dots)<br /> <b>oo -</b> +2 Supernatural Merit dots (5 dots)<br /> <b>ooo - </b>+2 Supernatural Merit dots (7 dots)<br /> <b>oooo - </b>+2 Supernatural Merit dots (9 dots)<br /> <b>ooooo - </b>+3 Supernatural Merit dots (12 dots)<br /> However, it is worth pointing out that Theurgy and Thaumaturgy simply widens the potential pool of Merits that Cultists can choose from, they don\u2019t directly <i>grant</i> those Merits. A given Cultist still only has a number of Supernatural Merits as per Second Sight, which is to say, three dots.<br /> <br /> <b>Building the Pyramid- Storytelling, Players Guide, and Crossover</b><br /> <br /> Cults can get a lot of mileage in the Chronicles of Darkness- they can stand in for just about any organization, and as you can see, come with a wide variety of options. So, how does one go about using a Cult in a story? What can it add to the game? Firstly, it fleshes out the setting. A Cult comes already packed with backstory, motivation, and plot hooks and it can help abstract the power of various factions at play in a given Chronicle. Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives are fleshed out characters in their own right, significant NPCs that act as the public face of the Cult and who make great antagonists or allies. If you\u2019re feeling really ambitious, a PC could actually be a Conspirator, Agent, or Operative. Technically speaking, if you\u2019re a member of one of the various social Y-Splats, your character sort of already is.<br /> <br /> It was mentioned above, but it bears repeating here, a single Cult, regardless of Reach or Grasp, isn\u2019t going to be able to completely take over a country. They can take over a branch of government, rule a small town, or something along those lines, but it will almost never take down an entire nation or something like a Hunter Conspiracy. Why? Those institutions are more than just a single Cult, they\u2019re nested Cults. Imagine if you would, trying to take over Canada. There are ten provinces and three territories, plus one federal government. That\u2019s fourteen Advanced Cults working together to govern a nation, just on the bureaucratic side. It\u2019s a lot more if you factor in other agencies, police forces, military branches, etc. I don\u2019t care how big your single Cult is, it is never going to take that down alone. This aspect of such massive Cults can be termed The Pyramid. The most powerful Cult in the organization sits at the top, and it branches out into smaller and smaller groups below it.<br /> <br /> As an example, I\u2019ll use the Deva Corporation, a far-reaching God-Machine Cult.<br /> <br /> Deva Corporation: <i>God-Machine Manual</i> Research/New Mason Architecture: Luminous Labs/Butterfly Cryptozoology, LTD/The Apocalypse Clock<br /> <br /> As another example, Last Dynasty International, a corporation hunting the Deathless<br /> <br /> Last Dynasty International: Last Dynasty Pharmaceuticals: Killwarden Memorial Conservation Fund/Integrated Transport Solutions, LLC<br /> <br /> Each of the above boxes represents a single, far-reaching Advanced Cult, with Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives fleshing out its membership, giving them more power in particular cities and particular sectors. Either way, in order to get through to the core Cult behind either of these continent spanning occult Enterprises you\u2019ll need to Investigate, or manipulate, no less than three powerful Cults, after having Investigated or manipulated your way through to an actual Conspirator, Agent, or Operative in one of the base Cults. Considering those high-ranking Agents and Operatives are likely to have their own Agents and Operatives, climbing to the top of the Pyramid is no easy task, and confronting those in charge of one of those organizations is a task worthy of a Chronicle. Much like with Investigation, you\u2019ll want to plan out how long the Chronicle to climb the Pyramid will be based on how many Clues you can gather. This style of play won\u2019t necessarily be for everyone, since it means uncovering layer upon layer of conspiracy and cover-up, but for some players and Storytellers Climbing the Pyramid is something that will make an enjoyable Chronicle, the kind that Demon: The Descent and Mage: The Awakening were made to handle. It\u2019s worth noting here, however, that a Mage Chronicle climbing the Pyramid is probably going to be much quicker than a Demon Chronicle climbing the Pyramid, if only because Mage Sight can make Investigation superfluous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 04-06-2016, 03:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T06:44:23-07:00","id":861007,"title":"","content":"More stuff, this time on making Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives characters. I'm getting a lot of mileage out of this, but I'm already foreseeing Werewolf being a real sticking point- figuring out how powerful an individual Pack is will be... interesting. Mummy will, naturally, be the most insane and fun, with Demon and Mage a close second. The way Changeling will interact with Cults will likely be very weird.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <u>Brick by Brick- Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives</u><br /> <br /> Whether you\u2019re building a Cult, or fighting against one, the public face of the Cult is always its outside contacts. The power of a Cult might be fairly limited, but a well-placed Conspirator can improve it several times over. Conspirators are blackmailed outsiders, true believer converts, bribed officials, traitors, and embedded contacts- when purchasing a Conspirator, think about who they are, and how they got there, not only will this help flesh out the <i>character</i>, but it will help establish guidelines as to how the Cult operates. Do they threaten those they want in positions of power? Do they send loyal members into gravely dangerous situations? Do they seduce and convert? Do they bribe?<br /> <br /> A Conspirator isn\u2019t, generally, an active character. They skulk in the shadows, they wield their limited influence, and they try and keep their heads down. If they refuse to talk to one of the PCs, are they frightened of retribution, wanting to keep their job, or does their master genuinely command their respect? Are they an irritating evangelist, constantly trying to persuade the party to join them? Do they believe in their mission, and consider the party a means to an end, or a threat? Do they see the PCs as a threat to their position?<br /> <br /> An Agent is much more proactive. An Agent is more than just an extension of the Cult, but a power in and of themselves. They still owe their allegiance and their authority to their masters, but they have built up <i>a lot</i> more pull. An Agent can act alone, boldly, with the power and influence of a small Cult themselves. In some cases, a successful Agent might be <i>as powerful</i> as their parent Cult, in which case the risk of them going rogue is constantly on their master\u2019s mind, but the Cult <i>needs</i> the power and influence of the Agent, and probably has several more. An Agent makes a good reoccurring antagonist- putting a face to the Cult paired with the power to make them an active threat, instead of just a looming shadow in the background. When designing an Agent, give a little more thought to their personal motivations and goals, because you can bet that they have an agenda beyond what their Cult asks of them. Alternatively, they could be a company man through and through, a true believer, loyal to a fault to their parent organization. When you need a starter antagonist to introduce a wider threat, an Agent is your best bet.<br /> <br /> An Operative is a bit different; an Operative is an Agent who\u2019s all grown up. If the Agent is the face of the man in the shadows, the operative <i>is</i> the man in the shadows. Think of the difference between any number of \u2018mythology\u2019 bad guys on X Files and Cancer Man. It\u2019s a bad time when one of the grand conspiracy\u2019s minions show up, but when Cancer Man arrives, <i>everything</i> hits the fan. The Operative is a Cult and a tour-de-force all on their own, with the resources and capabilities to back it up. If they\u2019re still attached to their parent Cult, there needs to be a reason. Maybe they\u2019re only formally associated, but the original organization maintains only distant contact, maybe the Operative needs the Cult to help them fulfill a personal goal, maybe maintaining ties with the Cult is a matter of convenience. Perhaps the Operative is genuinely <i>loyal</i> to the Cult. Either way, if you\u2019re going with a bigger story, or establishing a Chronicle climbing the Pyramid, then it\u2019s an Operative you want dogging the protagonist\u2019s steps to let them know they\u2019re in <i>way </i>over their heads.<br /> <br /> <i>Agents &amp; Operatives as Player Characters</i><b>:</b> Can you <i>play</i> an Agent or Operative? Sure, no reason not to. The only thing separating an Operative from being a fully-fledged character is the decision to make them one. Mechanically, an Agent or Operative is a relatively specific kind of play- you answer to a superior, you undertake missions from your organizations, but you also get a really rather nice Experience boost to begin the game. This really isn\u2019t all that different than playing a regular character involved in Covenant/Order/Tribe/Court politics, except that as an Agent or Operative you have a few more mechanical strings and boons attached.<br /> <br /> <i>Agents &amp; Operatives as Supernatural Creatures</i><b>:</b> In non-mortal game, or a game where you are using Cult mechanics to flesh out different supernatural organizations, Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives are likely to be members of the appropriate kind of esohuman (thanks Mage Chronicler\u2019s Guide for the awesome term). In that case, still follow the rules for Retainer when roughly determining their Skills, but take a look at the Ghoul Retainer rules in <b>Blood &amp; Smoke: The Strix Chronicles</b>. Following that same yardstick would be beneficial. What that translates out to in terms of power (for games currently updated to 2E) is outlined here below:<ul><li><b>Vampire: </b>Conspirator (1 dot of Disciplines), Agent 1-2 (2 dots of Disciplines) Agent 3 (3 dots of Disciplines). Spend Merit dots from Operative on Disciplines, Devotions, Blood Potency, and Merits.</li> <li><b>Werewolf:</b> Conspirator (1 Gift), Agent 1-2 (2 Gifts), Agent 3 (3 Gifts). Spend Merit dots from Operative on Gifts, Facets, Renown, Primal Urge, and Merits.</li> <li><b>Changeling: </b>Conspirator (3 dots of Contracts), Agent 1-2 (6 dots of Contracts), Agent 3 (9 dots of Contracts). Spend Merit dots from Operative on Contracts, Wyrd, and Merits.</li> <li><b>Demon:</b> Conspirator (1 Embed/Exploit and a Merit), Agent 1-2 (3 Embeds/Exploits), Agent 3 (4 Embeds/Exploits and a Merit). Spend Merit dots from Operative on Embeds/Exploits, Primum, Merits, and Cover.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T15:57:23-07:00","id":861390,"title":"","content":"Any chance you could write up some rules or advice for multiple people buying up Cult together?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/477-xenorath","name":"Xenorath","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"169"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T19:19:22-07:00","id":861528,"title":"","content":"That is something I'm absolutely going to cover, once I'm done the crossover section. I'm trying to get a piece in on how every Y-Splat handles Cults, but depending on the gameline, X-Splat would be more useful going in depth over. For example, in Requiem, it's fairly easy to figure out what kind of Cult different Clans would wield, but it will probably be more productive to talk about the Covenants. I'll probably go over the gamelines again and handle aspects I hadn't before when I get the chance.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <u>What We Do in the Shadows- Crossover</u><br /> <br /> And now, a brief word on what each kind of esohuman generally does with Cults.<br /> <br /> <b>Vampire:</b> While there could be dozens of reasons why a vampire would want a Cult, ultimately it all boils down to one thing in particular- blood. Almost the first thing young Kindred will use their Cult for is as a Herd. Vampires favor Reach over Grasp, preferring to use Conspirators and Agents to handle the needs of upholding the First Tradition if they can\u2019t toe the line themselves. When designing a local Domain, think of how powerful the Prince is, and <i>how</i> they maintain their power. Do they delegate to capable Agents and Operatives, their courtiers? Do they wield raw influence? Do they lean on the Covenants? Do they mostly use servants outside the normal Kindred power structure, ghouling significant mortals? This will influence who the Prince is, and how they rule. One with less personal power is likely to be a perfect politician, dancing along a razor\u2019s edge to keep the sharks at each other\u2019s throats instead of theirs. One with <i>a lot</i> of personal power is liable to be a stereotypical tyrant Prince.<br /> <br /> Because the Kindred rely on mortal agents to do their bidding during daylight hours and to reinforce the First Tradition, most of their Cults tend to be Elite Clubs or Cults of Personality. This is no different for the Covenants. Each Covenant is designed to function like a massive Cult, though local branches will almost certainly be smaller. Traits dedicated to the Covenant itself are technically tagged to \u201cthe Covenant\u201d but practically they are the purview of the Covenant\u2019s local leader. Merits that rely on Covenant Status and access Covenant resources are effectively using Reach or Grasp to get what they want. Operatives of the Covenants are likely to be other high-ranking Vampires in its power structure, while Agents and Conspirators are either lower down on the chain, mortals under their thumb, or Ghouls. Consider, then, what the character of the Covenant is based on how their Cult is built.<br /> <br /> <i>Blood Will Out</i><b>:</b> Where are the mortal members in all this? As mentioned above, Reach and Grasp translates to <i>more</i> than just influence, it\u2019s membership and contacts made. Simply put, for the Kindred, mortals are usually set dressing- food, resources, somewhere in the background. It wouldn\u2019t be strange for there to be some silent humans, pulses racing, at Kindred functions- after all, everyone needs to eat and it\u2019s an uncharitable host who doesn\u2019t provide refreshments. However, for Cults with heavy amounts of Lore, it is not unusual to have several Ghoul Agents, or for the majority of mortal cultists to be Ghouls themselves.<br /> <br /> The <b>Invictus</b> tend to be very top-heavy Elite Clubs or Enterprises, with the central leader of the First Estate wielding personal influence to keep their subordinates down and wielding unlucky neonate Conspirators as tools to uphold the Traditions. But consider a city where the power at the top is shared, where the might of The Old Man leans heavily upon a few equally powerful counterparts- Operatives, all. In such situations, the halls of the Conspiracy of Silence are likely to be filled with a lot more politics and backstabbing than is normal, and the bourgeoisie, Versaille-esque rules fall by the wayside.<br /> <br /> The <b>Carthian Movement</b> is usually a Gang or Unorganized, a faceless mass of the politically radical dead and their living counterparts. The Revolution is unusual in that it <i>doesn\u2019t</i> use a lot of Reach, but instead favors Grasp. As an Unorganized Cult, that\u2019s generally not a problem, they can reorganize as they see fit, but in a Domain where the Revolution is a bloody one, the Carthians could be in trouble if they ever get into power. The Revolution doesn\u2019t mind empowering Agents or Operatives, it allows them to compensate for a general lack of access to the higher-ups in mortal and Kindred society, but should the face of the Movement \u2018sell out\u2019? Well, there\u2019s always the next Revolution.<br /> <br /> The <b>Ordo Dracul</b> is a very peculiar kind of Elite Club, and a doubly peculiar Covenant, because it is almost entirely uninterested in temporal power. Dragon politics generally revolve around <i>not</i> being elected to the position of Koaigon, but retaining enough power and seniority to continue their research and follow their personal goals. This is most likely because, unlike the other Covenants, the Ordo Dracul is a Mystery Cult, and certainly behaves as one. The base Cult, representing the power of the Koaigon, is probably rather small, with major Operatives being the leaders of the three branches of the Sworn at the local Chapterhouse.<br /> <br /> The <b>Circle of the Crone</b> is hard to pin down, but generally favors Tribal, Cult of Personality, and Elite Club structures. It is virtually impossible to define the Acolytes, because more than any other Covenant they are highly unique and individualistic in their beliefs. The things that unite them are a Mystery Cult structure, polytheistic and/or animistic inclinations, belief in a Mother of Monsters/Crone figure, and Cruac. Pay extremely close attention to Step 1 of building the Crone Cult, as that will be crucial to determining how to divide up the Merit dots. Because of the highly individualistic nature of the Acolytes, Operatives and Agents might be senior members of the Cult with <i>vastly</i> different beliefs than the core group of Acolytes.<br /> <br /> The <b>Lancea et Sanctum</b> can be as diverse in its structure as the Circle of the Crone, but are more uniform in terms of beliefs because unlike the Acolytes, they have holy books and an existing family of religions to piggy back off of. (Neopaganism and polytheistic reconstruction are generally too new and diverse to have long-established traditions) The Sanctified, more than any other Covenant except, maybe, the Invictus, deal with mortals. Interactions with mortals are an important part of the duties of the faith, and as a result, they are the second-most likely to possess mortal Conspirators and Agents behind the First Estate itself. This helps compensate for their generally small size as a Cult.<br /> <br /> <i>The Ones Who Can\u2019t Stop</i><b>:</b> Ghouls have an odd position in the Covenants. They are valued servants who are, typically, denied access to true positions of power due to their fundamentally human nature. Ghouls have the advantage of being able to move in daylight, while still being capable of wielding Disciplines. While unable to use Cruac, nothing stops them from wielding Theban Sorcery. They can move in the worlds of both mortal and vampire, and that gives them some serious power in the Danse Macabre. It is also worth pointing out that they are, more often than not, Vitae addicts blood bonded to their Regnant, valuable tools in the Danse for their master. For all the independent power of a Ghoul, that power still depends on the Kindred.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T03:50:51-07:00","id":861724,"title":"","content":"Very nice. I can't wait to see the others. Especially Prometheans. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> (Because while it may not seem like most can <i>have</i> a cult, I can easily seem them being <i>part</i> of a cult. For awhile, at least.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T06:09:47-07:00","id":861773,"title":"","content":"Promethean is <i>definitely </i>going to be weird. If any Promethean can safely possess a Cult though, it's likely to be a Galateid. To a certain quality of 'safe'. What's likely to take up more space in that section is a discussion of Alchemist Cults, and <i>Sublimatus </i>Cults. Perhaps even the occasional, very odd, Cult that worships a <i>qashmal</i>, the worship giving the Scintillating Flame more Pyros.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Demon: </b>The Unchained live in the dark place where government agencies, corporations, and religions turn into Cults with sinister agendas. They used to serve the biggest manipulator of them all, and now they wield Its own strategies against it. The Unchained are willing to pick up any tools at their disposal, but for obvious reasons lean towards Conspiracies. Demon Cults are miniscule compared to those of other esohumans, primarily to preserve Cover and slip beneath the notice of the God-Machine. A cunning outcast relies upon Conspirators and Leverage, routing them through different Covers. An absolutely <i>crazy</i> Demon might make <i>their Cover</i> a Conspirator/Agent/Operative. The primary use of a Cult is resources, access, and security for one of the Unchained.<br /> <br /> Agencies lean towards being a bit bigger than personal Cults, but are no less reliant upon their Conspirators, Agents, Operatives, or Leverage. The difference being their Agents and Operatives tend to be fellow Unchained or Stigmatics instead of regular humans. Free Agencies keep hold of a lot of Leverage, Temporal Agencies tend to stockpile Lore, and Insurgent Agencies maintain Arsenals and train Militant Agents. Pay special attention to Step 1 when designing an Agency, but also remember the inherently paranoid nature of the Unchained- how big can a Demon Cult get, how much trust can they invest in someone, before it becomes a security risk? What problems plague the Agency? How much attention are they willing to attract?<br /> <br /> The cults of <b>Tempters</b> are often bigger than what most Unchained would consider safe, but the hedonistic outcasts are terribly fond of their luxuries, and the Builders generally want the resources to construct their own Hell at the end of the Descent. Tempters favor Reach over Grasp, and will often go Legitimate, running Enterprises and Elite Clubs with shady Splinter Organizations often dedicated to supporting a Temporal Agency, supporting the primary Cult off the books, or investigating the possibility of an off-world colony in which to establish their Hell. The Builders are all about resources, contacts, and Leverage, and often possess massive webs of fairly low-importance Conspirators.<br /> <br /> The <b>Saboteurs</b> are almost always running Conspiracies or Gangs, possibly both at once, and are generally a bit louder than most Unchained would like. The Insurgent Agencies they regularly turn into can never keep a lucky streak going forever. Saboteurs favor Grasp, Arsenals, and Militant cultists- they go out of their way to find Stigmatic malcontents who want to give a two-fingered salute to the Machine. Unfortunately, this usually gets them on the radar of law enforcement, which in turn sets the God-Machine on their trail. Leverage, Agents, and Operatives continue to be a Demon\u2019s best friend for hiding in the shadows, though the Thugs tend to favor quality over quantity.<br /> <br /> It is a remarkable feat even <i>hearing</i> about the Conspiracies of the <b>Inquisitors</b>. They\u2019re paranoid, even for Demons, and double down on layers and layers of security. The Watcher at the center of this spider\u2019s web is the head of a small Cult with a veritable army of Conspirators who have no idea who they work for. Occasionally, they\u2019ll set the ball rolling on an Unorganized internet-based Cult encouraging conspiracy theorists and groups like Network Zero and sits back- vigilantly -to watch the data pour in. The Watcher hides themselves behind computer screens and chat rooms, maybe dedicating the resources to make an Agent or two, but rarely, if ever, meeting in person. Giving someone resources means keeping a closer eye on them, and the hit will be bigger when the knife goes into the Paranoid\u2019s back.<br /> <br /> No one really <i>likes</i> an <b>Integrator</b>, but they can understand them. The other outcasts might not agree, but no one can deny that an Idealist\u2019s heartfelt faith and simple desire to return home is something they felt at one time or another\u2026 before they knew better. Generally speaking, an Integrator\u2019s faith <i>is</i> honest, even if it puts other Demons in danger. More than any other Agenda, their Cults are likely to be Tribal as their faith in the loyalists and in the Machine verges on the religious. The Cults of Turncoats are generally rather visible, but that\u2019s the idea- grab the attention of the Machine. Of course, most Integrators want to return on their own terms, so they aren\u2019t likely to go public at first, but once they do it\u2019s only a matter of time before the Cult is co-opted by God.<br /> <br /> The God-Machine has no such inclination, It uses whatever tool is best suited for the job, and while small and unobtrusive is usually Its design, sometimes brute force levels of influence are more what the situation calls for. Cults are a means to fuel the activity of Angels and a way to maintain Infrastructure and act without the kind of Aetheric Resonance an Angel gives off. Exiles, on the other hand, tend to be reliant upon Cults as access to Essence- otherwise they would have to bargain with their loyal counterparts for the opportunity to use Infrastructure.<br /> <br /> <i>Those Who Can See</i><b>:</b> Stigmatics are the wild cards in this cold war between the Machine and its rebellious servants, recruited, groomed, created, and conscripted by each individual side for their power and knowledge. A Stigmatic\u2019s identity isn\u2019t at risk acting in an unusual manner, they can take risks the Unchained wouldn\u2019t, and they don\u2019t trigger Aetheric Resonance, making them the perfect infiltration assets for Angels. This puts a Stigmatic, <i>any</i> Stigmatic, under a lot of pressure to join one side or another, and the players in this game don\u2019t have to play nicely. Stigmatics are indoctrinated, infested with programmable cryptoflora, embedded with enough wetware to make them a Lost Boy, conditioned with Numina and technology to become a Dreamer, or even worse fates. Stigmatics need to take decisive action, and they always need to do it fast. Remember rule number one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T07:24:21-07:00","id":861817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=861773#post861773\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Promethean is <i>definitely </i>going to be weird. If any Promethean can safely possess a Cult though, it's likely to be a Galateid. To a certain quality of 'safe'. What's likely to take up more space in that section is a discussion of Alchemist Cults, and <i>Sublimatus </i>Cults. Perhaps even the occasional, very odd, Cult that worships a <i>qashmal</i>, the worship giving the Scintillating Flame more Pyros.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually have as one of my NPC character concepts a Promethean Crime Boss Mastermind. He uses Transhuman Potential to ramp up his Intelligence for crafting Plans, then communicates with his various agents and minions using intermediaries, dead-drop, and, more recently, IMs and Email. His criminal network would be <i>perfectly</i> represented by the Cult rules here (with heavy use of Agents and Operatives).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:14:05-07:00","id":861938,"title":"","content":"Now <i>that </i>sounds like something extremely interesting. I'd love to see you take a crack at writing it up, if only because I'd like to see someone try and work the system I'm developing. In any case, here's my Changeling stuff.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling:</b> The Lost have an\u2026 interesting relationship with Cults. The attitude of most Cults often make them think of their Durance, of their servitude to the Gentry, and quite a few Lost would bristle at the comparison between the organizations they establish and the courts of Arcadia. But, the comparisons still stand. The Courts are the big ones, acting as support structures for their members with a heavy ritual aspect. There are <i>excellent</i> reasons for this, notably that it protects the city housing the Freehold, making it virtually impossible for the True Fae to act, and extremely tempting for any Huntsmen to follow the rules, but if people were to know the extent of their activities, the Courts would still look like Cults. The Entitlements even more so, being the more exclusive and even more ritualized groups that they are. The Lost are on the long road home, but they can also never go back. Faerie has etched its laws into their soul, and part of those laws is rite and ritual, and only like-minded individuals can understand.<br /> <br /> Consider the story of a Court, their Approaches, and how that influences their structure. Who are their Conspirators? What kind of Leverage do they hold? Does the Court have Home Turf in the form of a communal Hollow? Do they have an Agent in certain organizations to help the Hedge-fresh gain new identities and get back on their feet? Do they have access to weaponry? Do they bribe the local Goblin Market for first crack at their goods? The Courts and Entitlements of the Lost are still Cults by definition, but they are generally benevolent ones. This isn\u2019t to say that they are perfectly law-abiding or unattached to criminal elements- Faerie knows no law save the Wyrd, and the Lost have to play by those rules. The darker side of humanity can be an excellent source of Glamour, and the desperate are always ready for a Bargain. The Cults of the Lost are support structures first and foremost, and that means access to Glamour, Tokens, and mortals they can Bargain with in order to hide from the Huntsmen.<br /> <br /> The <b>Fairest</b> are inclined towards Cults of Personality, sometimes without the intention of starting them. They simply produce a following where they go due to their inspiring nature. The natural inclination towards leadership, towards nobility, emerges in their Cults. They try and inspire others to be the best they can be. Some Fairest with Cults are often motivational speakers with a following, public figures trying to make the world a better place, but that doesn\u2019t stop them from indulging in the darker side of Cults too. Sometimes, a Fairest just needs to feel like they\u2019re in charge. The Fairest fiercely defend their followers, as they can\u2019t stand for any of them to come to harm- it tears at their Clarity with iron talons and hurts their confidence as well.<br /> <br /> The <b>Darklings</b> had their loyalty betrayed and their ideals crushed, and the supposedly simple matters of \u201cright\u201d and \u201cwrong\u201d won\u2019t hold them back anymore. Darkling Cults favor Grasp, which generally puts them in the wheelhouse of Conspiracies. It <i>hurts</i> a Darkling to trust someone else blindly, but they thrive off of making difficult choices for others- even if they don\u2019t know it. An enemy of the Court vanishes, a televised criminal is found dead, some Hedge-fresh Changeling goes missing and the Darkling slaps a Token of Reprieve into a motley-mate\u2019s hand with the implicit understanding \u2018Don\u2019t ask\u2019<br /> <br /> An <b>Ogre</b> speaks with their fists, trying to hide the pain that drove them to try and become invulnerable to begin with. Ogre Cults lean towards Gangs, and that\u2019s generally just how they like it. More so even than a Gang, the Cults of Ogres are violent, verging into something like a fight club. Of course, this can be channeled into something productive- like a martial arts group, or something along those lines, but more often than not it\u2019s something a little more sinister. The Freehold with an Ogre Cult Leader can depend on them to bring highly effective head-smashing thugs to a fight when they need them to. However, the Cult of an Ogre is often reactionary, lashing out against perceived harm and any reminder of vulnerability.<br /> <br /> The <b>Wizened</b> are comfortable amongst Enterprises, and those are the Cults they build and lead. Propped up by supernatural skill and Contracts, a Wizened-run business is likely to become <i>extremely</i> successful, but probably won\u2019t. The Wizened are perfectionists- anything less than their absolute best smashes a hole through their Clarity, and as such they will not tolerate sub-par workmanship. More likely than not, a Wizened-run Enterprise is likely to be a highly respected, but very small, artisanal shop. Of course, whatever it is the Wizened specializes in, the Freehold can expect the very best of it.<br /> <br /> Here\u2019s what a <b>Beast</b> cares about<b></b>: family, friends, the herd, the pack, the Freehold. Here\u2019s what they <i>don\u2019t</i> care about<b></b>: the laws and traditions of human society. Beasts break cages, real or imagined; they test limits and hone their skills to perfection. A Beast isn\u2019t unintelligent just because they\u2019re animalistic- physicality can be intimately tied to cunning. The Cults of a Beast are Tribal, usually small and tight-knit, with recognized Home Turf. They\u2019re rarely legitimate, if only for the fact that they could care less which rules they have to break in order to feel free. The Freehold can always depend on a Beast\u2019s Cult to have a place to fall back to, a territory they know intimately where they can run circles around their enemies.<br /> <br /> The Cults of <b>Elementals</b> can be as sturdy as stone or as mercurial and changing as the winds- or perhaps they shift with the tides. These are Unorganized Cults, focused on an ideal, a platonic form, as embodied in the Elemental. They change when the Changeling says they change, when the circumstances necessitate that they change. The Elemental will justify to themselves, and to their Cultists, when they can. If things go well, then it was all part of a grander plan. The Freehold can depend on an Elemental\u2019s Cult to be whatever they need it to be- a steady rock on which to fall, a mine full of resources, or a crashing storm against their foes. Even a modestly sized Elemental Cult can swing to extremes with ease.<br /> <br /> The Gentry can benefit from Cults as well, subtly influencing them through a Prop, speaking to them through their Wisps, seducing them away to Faerie or bullying them into gathering up soon-to-be slaves. Perhaps the Old God is even whispering to them from beyond the grave, through one of the Inamorata. Maybe the Stranger has ordered a Huntsman to command the Cult for them- an angry, cunning, vicious creature willing to do whatever it needs to in order to go back home. A group of privateers or Loyalists is just as much a Cult as a Goblin Market or Hob-tribe. This brings in an important factor of Changeling life- political concerns aren\u2019t just on <i>one</i> side of the Hedge. If the Freehold needs to negotiate with a nearby Hedgetown or Hob-Kingdom, they\u2019ll do what they need to do. More so than almost any other esohuman, a Changeling is part of two highly distinct worlds, and can disappear fully into one or the other, and that is dangerous regardless of whether they choose Faerie or Ironside.<br /> <br /> <i>The Oath Keepers</i><b>:</b> The Fae-Touched <i>are home</i>, they can go back whenever they want to; family, friends, a job, a home, it\u2019s all there for them. But most of them don\u2019t want to go back, because the people at home won\u2019t understand what they\u2019ve seen, what they\u2019ve been through. The Lost can understand, the Lost can help them get through it, but even then the Lost have been through <i>so much more</i>. The Lost don\u2019t want them involved any more than they have to, regardless of how much they might want them to be, either because the Fae-Touched can\u2019t <i>really</i> understand, because they don\u2019t want them involved in the society the Lost have built for themselves. Whatever the reasons, Fae-Touched <i>crave</i> the company of Changelings, partially because they need someone who they can relate to, but also because they have seen much of the wonder, and not enough of the horror, of Faerie. The Fae-Touched are living on borrowed time, wanting to plunge headfirst into a world that could swallow them whole. They can be liabilities as much as they are assets.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:46:54-07:00","id":861962,"title":"","content":"Damn.... OwO<br /> <br /> Let me just say (hands down) that you have quite the impressive piece of work unfolding in this thread. So far, it's your write-ups of the Cults with respect to supernatural splats that are my favorites, so far. With Changeling being the number one of them all.<br /> <br /> Keep up the good work, man <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806810-deionscribe-s-homebrew-hub#post806810\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;My Homebrew Hub&quot;</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\" target=\"_blank\">Age of Azar</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/907224-the-kingdom-of-yamatai-dark-era\" target=\"_blank\">The Kingdom of Yamatai</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","name":"Deionscribe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T11:45:31-07:00","id":863244,"title":"","content":"High praise, thank-you. And now, the bit that's probably the most intuitive to write, Mummies. Promethean will follow.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Mummy:</b> Cults are the lifeblood of the Deathless. Without them, only the turn of Sothis and certain Affinities can wake them from the death-state of <i>henet</i>. A Mummy\u2019s primary means of mortal interaction will be with members of their Cult, and that makes designing that organization a vital part of any Mummy game. When designing a Cult, remember that for all the fact they serve the Deathless, some Cultists will spend entire lives without seeing their immortal god and master. Think of the Purpose the Mummy founded the Cult for; consider the Relic the Mummy uses the Cult to guard, and think about how the culture they\u2019ve grown up in influences their Doctrine. They may, nominally, worship the Nameless Beasts and the Judges, but only the most fanatical and devout will adhere strictly to those beliefs in a culture that doesn\u2019t worship them themselves.<br /> <br /> Something you might want to think about is how long ago the Mummy founded the Cult, and what\u2019s happened since then. Even if you\u2019ve established the Purpose, Relic, and Doctrine of the Cult with the Storyteller, consider giving the Storyteller the freedom to change and warp the Purpose and Doctrine as time has gone on. The Cult might worship the Mummy, but the Deathless god isn\u2019t constantly around to maintain tradition. Between different Descents, the Mummy might even wake up to a Rogue Cult, a Civil War, or other forms of internal strife, and one faction or another is liable to have summoned them to handle concerns they couldn\u2019t themselves. While a Cult will maintain itself in the absence of their master, without certain Affinities they won\u2019t <i>expand</i> without the say-so of the Deathless god.<br /> <br /> A Mummy\u2019s Cult is deeply personal, their Purpose and Doctrine influenced by the personal history of the Deathless, the traditions and symbols of the Guilds, the Nameless Beast that stands in for their Decree, and the Mummy\u2019s own Judge. As much as a Scorpion Cult is an independent organization, it\u2019s also an extension of the Mummy\u2019s own character. Taking the time to flesh out Purpose and Doctrine will elaborate on the Cult\u2019s backstory, and the kind of master the Mummy once was. In this way, the Cult functions a lot like a vampire\u2019s Touchstone, reminding them of who they were, and who they are- hints towards their Memory. Of course, if it\u2019s been a while since their last Descent, garbled traditions and doctored histories could lead the Mummy in the wrong direction, and they might not like what they find if it differs greatly from what they\u2019ve been told.<br /> <br /> The <b>Lion-Headed</b> are impetuous, tempestuous, making the most of their short time on earth by indulging in all-too human passion. They make enemies and friends with incredible ease, swift to be offended and impressed, with a reputation for rash decisions and hopeless causes. They are animated, charismatic, and passionate people, with bright eyes and emphatic gestures. This sheer force of personality swiftly endears them to mortals, who fascinate them to the point of encouraging the Hands of Sekhmet to delay (or even abandon) their summoned Purpose. Their Cults are often Cults of Personality, rising to exceptional heights when their Deathless master wakes, and collapsing to a shell of their former might with their return to Duat. Such cults are built on personal loyalty, passed down through generations, in sects that might be a small family following (something like a 1-dot Cult) or a religion of hundreds (an Advanced Cult).<br /> <br /> The <b>Falcon-Headed</b> want tough, competent, self-sufficient Cultists. They favor Reach and Grasp equally, one representing aspiration, the other competence. They invest heavily in Agents and Operatives, rewarding their most loyal and accomplished servants with power and increased responsibility. Their inclination towards conquering trials often pushes the Charioteers to make their organizations particularly brutal Mystery Cults, with initiation involving things like surviving in the wild for a week. Their Foundations are generally Conspiracies, and the terse, improvisational, driven nature of Talons means they can be a bit short on their doctrine. This combination of Mystery Cult or Conspiracy means that the Charioteer will drive their Cultists to the breaking point, and at the end of that tunnel they\u2019ll expect to find elaborate mythologies, rituals, and taboos that will grant <i>meaning</i> to their suffering. If the Mummy can\u2019t provide, the Cult may revolt.<br /> <br /> The <b>Bull-Headed</b> foster the largest Cults of any of the Deathless, leaning heavily on Reach. (A little grasp might be needed, but they generally believe they\u2019re doing the right thing and that society should respect their insights and dedication.) Most are Tribal Cults blatantly constructed as religions, so that their master can bask in their rituals and total obedience, but in time some have evolved into expansive Enterprises. The Millstones are spectacularly dedicated, either to a core principle or to themselves, and in either case loyalty to fellow Deathless, ethics, their masters, and more or less the entire world, comes second to that essential aspect of themselves. They are reliable and stalwart, if you\u2019re on the same side. Otherwise, the Millstones will use their reputation for honesty and devotion to place Conspirators, gain Leverage, and otherwise destroy their rivals with disciplined imagination and ruthless thoroughness.<br /> <br /> The <b>Serpent-Headed</b> tend to mid-sized Cults, larger than some, smaller than most. These immortal intellectuals often attract like-minded individuals, store up copious amounts of Lore, and plant Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives at key points in the antiquities business to keep an eye on any number of vessels and hints towards their own past. They tend to recruit through those channels as well, using Agents and Operatives to seek out archaeologists with an eye for Relics, retrieve any vessels they might have found, and put them on the payroll. These Cults are Elite Clubs for archaeologists and anthropologists, sometimes with genuine Legitimacy as organizations dedicated to preserving antiquities. (\u201cThat belongs in a museum!\u201d Well, technically, it\u2019s a Tomb. But sure) In other cases, they might be Tribal Mystery Cults, an almost monastic religion dedicated to the pursuit of higher knowledge and/or the appeasement of entities that knowledge might unleash.<br /> <br /> The <b>Jackal-Headed</b> are patrons to the smallest Cults amongst the Deathless, Gangs or Tribes with a Mystery Cult structure and an odd philosophy of nihilism and restraint. The Ferrymen rarely have anything in common with mortals, and it takes an odd group of mortals comfortable with death to deal with the self-proclaimed masters of passage between the lands of the living and the dead. What few Cultists they possess are armed, trained, empowered as Agents and Operatives- they are doomsday enthusiasts and death cultists, ready for the end of the world. They tend to be isolated, invested heavily in fortified Home Turf. The Cults of Anpu\u2019s Torches generally end up looking <i>exactly</i> like most people imagine a Cult to appear, the difference being that these men, women, and children will all bow to the will of their Deathless master, even if it leads them to follow their god down into death.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Shuankhsen make use of whatever Cults they can get their hands on- picking up the disenfranchised, the rebellious, and the downtrodden. Their Cults tend to be Unorganized, able to transform into anything they need them to be. They Compartmentalize, turning isolated groups of the Cult into fodder for Ammut\u2019s hunger and fuel for the Fasad. They like to track the Arisen through Conspirators and Agents implanted in the Cults of their sworn enemies, and because the kings amongst the Lifeless have a much higher chance of earning a Descent Reset (by committing terrible acts, admittedly), increasing the periods they can endure. Paired with their perfect memories, a Cult headed by a Shuankhsen is quite able to predict the actions of their Arisen nemeses long before their enemy can even remember who they are.<br /> <br /> The Deceived don\u2019t generally invest in Reach, seeing as their use of Enigma actively impairs that particular trait, and so they pour all their resources into Grasp and Lore. When the Lost Guild needs to move in places of power, they invest powerful Agents and Operatives, allowing them to compensate for the shroud of their master\u2019s protection. These Agents and Operatives are not full members of the Cult, but merely answer to them- joining in the mystical bond of worshipping one of the Deceived would render the advantage of their Reach moot. As a result, the Cults of Deceived with high Enigma ratings rule over <i>massive</i> Mystery Cult Conspiracies, stuffed to the gills with Lore and power, and armed to the teeth. They are ghosts and mysteries even to those who trade in secrets and conspiracies. They are the secret, horrifying masters of the world, concealed in the darkness, announced by servants both rich and powerful who are excluded from such exalted company. To the Arisen and Lifeless who know of their existence, they are horrors among horrors, gods even to the greatest of monsters.<br /> <br /> <i>The Witnesses</i><b>:</b> Those who live in the presence of gods are cursed and blessed beyond imagining. They are wracked by the throes of Sybaris, killed at the whims of their gods, but they can\u2019t help but serve. Sybaris won\u2019t let them go, belief and loyalty (and fear) keeps them in line, and in the case of the worshippers of the Deceived, they literally <i>cannot</i> stop without a monumental act of will. Witnesses have it worse, seeing the Deathless and Lifeless in their true horror and glory, minds unbent by Sybaris, but obsessed eternally by the lure and power of Sekhem. Mortals in the path of Sekhem and Irem\u2019s ruins can go years, decades, possibly lifetimes without even seeing the god they worship. Picture the High Priest, used to uncontested power for years, forced to bow before a god they didn\u2019t- couldn\u2019t -know was a literal, physical being. Think of the Cultists who joined to be rebellious, or were part of a Conspiracy or Enterprise for meaning. Think of the Cultist who joined the wrong club. At the end of that pursuit of meaning, at the end of the search for truth, they find only curses and the horrifying, glorious power of the Tomb.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T03:45:00-07:00","id":863554,"title":"","content":"Arcanist, may i please use some elements of your Cult merit in the fansplat i'm working on?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T04:05:10-07:00","id":863558,"title":"","content":"Someone should write the Order of Silent Hill using these rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2411-raistlin","name":"Raistlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"713"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T07:59:56-07:00","id":863650,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ajf115</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=863554#post863554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Arcanist, may i please use some elements of your Cult merit in the fansplat i'm working on?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> You most certainly can. This system, like the Pact system I posted a while back, are meant to be used by anyone so long as it improves the fun at their gaming table. I'm trying to make this as user-friendly as possible, but I'm still not too sure if it is just yet. Anyways, Promethean stuff now.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <b>Promethean: </b>Empowered by a force of irresistible change that forms the foundation of the human soul, called down from the heavens by madness, passion, genius, and obsession. Were it not for the way it burns the earth and scorches minds and souls, Azoth would be perfect to center a cult around. It is immortal, transcendent, purifying\u2026 destructive. Pyros changes all it touches, for good or ill, and it has a history of inspiring those obsessive, brilliant, or mad enough to try and call it down and control it. Pyros isn\u2019t matter, energy, or an idea. It comes in substances, notably Vitriol and the alchemical compounds infusing Promethean flesh. It isn\u2019t energy, even though it radiates out with a measureable aura and has a tangible effect on those in its field. Pyros possesses a philosophical angle, which is what creates Azothic Memory. The alchemical reactions of the Divine Fire aren\u2019t just physical or chemical, but emotional, metaphorical, and spiritual. It changes and purifies its subject, but does not consume that which it changes. It can, at worst, reduce it down to become something else.<br /> <br /> Disquiet can make supporting a Cult <i>very</i> difficult for a Promethean, depending on the Lineage. In other cases, the proper collection of Transmutations and judicious use of Conspirators, Agents, and Operatives can make it more than possible; it can actually make it entirely sustainable. The question is: why? What would drive a Promethean to start a Cult? Perhaps it is an attempt to bring people close. Perhaps they want to understand the drive to worship. Maybe it\u2019s entirely unintentional- the Disquiet of some Prometheans can make that all too likely, turning the unwitting Created into an unwilling idol. In rare cases, the Promethean might believe themselves to be truly divine, given the right to rule by the Azoth that gives them life.<br /> <br /> Despite appearances the <b>Wretched</b> are actually idealists; their choleric humour may be fuelled by pain, sparked by anger, fanned by restlessness and irritability, but the torch lit from that flame could be turned to idealistic ends. They can be doctrinaires, enthusiastic and inflexible in their devotion. Their passion verges on the dogmatic and fanatical, but the consequences for a Wretched who finds their faith misplaced can be catastrophic. One goes into deep denial, another spirals into nihilistic depression, and a third can become equally passionate in pursuing a cause diametrically opposed to the original. A Wretched Centimanus can be one of the most sincere monsters you\u2019ll ever meet. This inclination can drive the Wretched to build up <i>any</i> Cult, but they\u2019re inclined towards Tribal and Enterprise Foundations. Because of the \u201cTorches and Pitchforks\u201d nature of the Wretched Disquiet, most Frankensteins who build up a Cult often develop Disquietism Transmutations to redirect that ire to more \u2018productive\u2019 targets, wielding it like a weapon.<br /> <br /> The <b>Galateids </b>are among the most likely to accidentally gather a Cult, typically a Cult of Personality, due to the nature of their Disquiet. The Muse\u2019s pursuit of mortality is, in many ways, ironic, because they possess beauty and power that people spend billions on. They cannot understand, or condone, their own inhuman perfection. The desires of the Galateid, fuelled by the Sanguine humour, are confused at best, blurring the lines between parent, child, friend, and lover. They are capable of astonishing courage and selflessness, but equally extreme obsession, lust, and bravado. Their Cults are little families they\u2019ve built up around themselves that they guard jealously, and they react badly to anyone attempting to leave. Make no mistake, their Disquiet can sour situations swiftly, but the Muse often <i>encourages</i> their Disquiet, bonding mortals to them with their Sanguine humour as surely as any Kindred blood doll\u2019s addiction or the use of Disciplines, and often to the same effect. It\u2019s entirely possible that the uninitiated might mistake a Muse for one of the Kindred.<br /> <br /> The <b>Nepri</b> build up Cults of Personality and Tribal afterlife cults. They are obsessed with mortal transitions, why people fear the loss of their body. To these immortals even amongst the extremely enduring Created, death, pain, and suffering is an idle curiosity. The Osirians are keenly aware of their divine progenitor, and hardly consider their body as being of any importance, seeing value only in the Azoth within. They can see themselves as distant from their fellow Created as they are from humanity, and they want to understand <i>why</i>- asking questions with a cold, unsettling, overeager curiosity. Their Disquiet and their Disfigurements remind people of impending death, inspiring a single emotion that flares dangerously when they are reminded of the transitory nature of life, when they feel as though their time is being wasted. A Nepri Cult Leader is an expert in taking this Disquiet and exploiting it, making it seem as though they have the answers, returning time and again from beyond the veil of death. The inexperienced will often confuse them with the Deathless, though not without good reason.<br /> <br /> The <b>Tammuz</b> are the <i>other</i> Lineage inclined to \u2018accidentally\u2019 picking up Cults due to the curse of their Disquiet. Those Cults tend to be Elite Clubs, as those controlled by the Golem\u2019s Disquiet get a perverse joy in the powerful slave they\u2019ve taken hostage. In other cases, the Cult takes on a different character, as the Disquiet driving them to possess the Tammuz instead encourages them to take advantage of the Promethean\u2019s goodwill and desire to save those humans who seem so fragile compared to the Created. But the Golems are themselves named for a dying-and-rising god, who had a far-reaching Cult in the ancient Near East. An important part of the myth of Tammuz is that he was sacrificed in Inanna\u2019s place, descending to the Underworld. While she was in wrath that he was found sitting on her throne when she returned, the weeping of the people for Tammuz, who gave so much to the people. The Golem may be encouraged to try and foster a Cult, then <i>endure</i> through Disquiet until they are torn to pieces by their worshippers, hopeful that their sacrifice will inspire the New Dawn. That is, unless their Torment gets to them first and they tear through their cultists.<br /> <br /> The <b>Ulgan</b> are ecstatics, shamans treading the boundary between mortal and spirit. These Cults are, typically, also Spirit Cults, of Tribal or Elite Club Foundations. The Riven accept their duty as walkers-between with a sort of grave dignity- shamans have always been outsiders amongst their tribes. The Spirits themselves tend to either rejoice or fear the effect of the Ulgan Wasteland, as Twilight becomes transparent and the Gauntlet starts to collapse. Because that kind of spiritual turmoil tends to attract the Uratha, and Disquiet already inspires murderous rage in <i>humans</i>, some of the Spirits will generally take it upon themselves to drive the Ulgan away, kill them, or protect them as it fits their goals and confidence in being able to handle the Uratha. Ulgan Cults are packed full of Lore, and they often see the Generative Act as an apprenticeship- selecting those talented in dealing with spirits for the dark ritual that will turn them into the Riven.<br /> <br /> Their opponents are no less inclined to the making and maintaining of Cults. Alchemists build them as a means to gather Pyros, find Prometheans (and with them, Athanors) and acquire resources. In rare cases, the <i>qashmallim</i> start them, for reasons as unusual and enigmatic as the Dominions themselves. Far more likely Cult Leaders are the <i>Sublimati</i>, the most unholy of the Mockeries; Pandorans elevated to human and transhuman levels of intelligence, without the desire or inclination to become human. Their unique conditions of sentience, fuelled as they are by a force of mutation and stagnation, leads to very strange conclusions and beliefs. They often found radical philosophies, moral systems, and even full-blown religions- with themselves hiding in the shadows due to the nature of their Disquiet. Unless, that is, like the Lady of Chains they develop unusual Dread Powers in order to circumvent that restraint. Some of them start organizations so powerful they don\u2019t just draw mortals in, but other <i>Sublimati</i> and Centimanus Prometheans as well.<br /> <br /> <i>Torchbearers</i><b>:</b> Humans caught in the path of the Divine Fire don\u2019t generally escape untouched. Azoth <i>forces</i> change, for good or ill, and the raw radiance of it scorches the land and ignites in human minds. Disquiet can affect a person long after the Promethean has departed, perpetuating cycles of change and unusual behaviour against their will. Alchemists and Demiurges, however, <i>deliberately</i> seek it out. Pyros didn\u2019t trigger a downward spiral (some would say it didn\u2019t trigger an <i>immediate</i> downwards spiral) but instead lit them aflame, making them capable of wielding the Divine Fire to perform miracles and unleash horrors. And amidst all this chaos and madness sparked by errant divinity the <i>qashmallim</i> drift, performing their inscrutable quests under the command of an ineffable master. Some men want to watch the world burn, others want to light it aflame. There\u2019s a subtle difference between the two.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-15T10:14:57-07:00","id":865993,"title":"","content":"And now, werewolves.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Werewolves: </b>A pack of Uratha isn\u2019t a terribly large organization, but it is undoubtedly a Cult. The rite and ritual demanded by Totem, Lodge, Protectorate, and Tribe is distinctly cultic. The psychology of the Uratha is equally divergent, and the importance of the pack looms so large in their minds and social lives that seeing pack members together almost <i>screams</i> \u2018Cult\u2019 to the casual observer. A Forsaken pack is, almost always, a Gang or Tribal Cult. A pack that leans more towards Tribal affiliations is a Tribal Cult, and is generally a good deal larger than a pack that is less strongly affiliated with the Tribes. Packs generally don\u2019t invest much into spreading the influence of the pack directly- temporal power and running a Cult isn\u2019t exactly their strong suit. When they <i>do</i> need wider influence, they generally invest in Wolf-Blooded Agents and Operatives, or invest greater power into their various packmates.<br /> <br /> Packs normally have a certain amount of Lore, generally enough to possess the Patron Merit, which acts as an extra three dots of the Totem Merit. The Second Sight Merit generally insures that every member of the pack possesses at least a dot in Totem. Some packs, in particular those with strong Fire-Touched and Bone Shadow connections, cultivate more Lore to build Cults tied to the core pack made up mostly of Wolf-Blooded, or mortals with Supernatural Merits intended to facilitate easier interactions with Spirits. On rare occasions, packs dabble in other Cult structures. Ivory Talons and Storm Lords have inclinations towards Elite Clubs and Cults of Personality, while Iron Masters occasionally branch out into Enterprises to compete with similarly adventurous Ivory Talon elite. The Predator Kings, if they bother to build a Cult at all, are generally very small, and very Isolated, with <i>significant</i> Home Turf. The scions of Dire Wolf would prefer to run in Pangaea reborn, thank-you very much.<br /> <br /> But far more, and far better, writers than myself have gone over the intricacies of Uratha packs- what are of more interest for the purpose of this work are Spirit Cults and packs solely comprised of Wolf-Blooded and humans. In such cases, the Wolf-Blooded become masters of Rites, learning them from Spirits and the careful observation of the Hisil and learning the flow of Essence and its laws. A Spirit Cult can be of any Foundation, but the nature of the Shadow generally pushes them to things that suit their nature. Tribal Cults and Unorganized Cults remain the most common, but every other Foundation sees some sort of representation amongst the worshippers of the Hisil. These Cults, for obvious reasons, often end up in conflict with the Uratha, though typically under very different motivations based on the circumstances. Some see themselves as \u2018monster hunters\u2019, others believe that they have as much a right to the Shadow as any werewolf, and some simply seek the occult secrets of the Spirits. In rare circumstances, the Uratha find some Spirit Cults comprised of nothing but humans, without even the Wolf-Blooded to support the Totem bond. Worryingly, in such Cults the Claimed seem to occupy a place of honor equivalent to the Uratha, and may practice Rites that call upon the Claiming Spirit\u2019s nature- Rites no werewolf has ever seen before.<br /> <br /> <i>Hosts of Foes</i><b>:</b> Spirit Cults are amongst the most dangerous of all the organizations the Uratha can come up against- just as capable, if not more so, of providing a hospitable environment for Spirits to operate, driven by intense personal motivations as well as the manipulations of the Spirits involved, they are capable of coming close to matching the mystical might of the Uratha. Just as frightening, if not more so, are Cults that spring up around the <i>shartha</i>. A parade of fresh host bodies for the Shards, allowing them to return time and again to plague local packs, and mortal resources that allow a potent Shard to expand their area of influence and find others of their kind. Persistent, terrifying, rumors indicate massive cults of potent Azlu Crones and Beshilu Ministers that destroy all who come before them and ceaselessly seek out the hearts of their Pangaean progenitors.<br /> <br /> Skinthieves are always a concern, finding their way into Spirit Cults and Uratha packs alike. They are generally harmless, to a certain quality of harmless, making good packmates- most are often Wolf-Blooded, or at the very least experienced occultists. In the unusual cases of a Skinthief capable of transforming into a Spirit or other Ephemeral Being, they make <i>especially</i> important pack members, capable of infiltrating the local Spirit Courts and assisting the Totem directly. In the case of an antagonistic Skinthief, there are few as dangerous. No one is quite sure what would happen if a Skinthief managed to kill and skin a werewolf, but no one really wants to find out.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-15T11:09:22-07:00","id":866018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=865993#post865993\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Ivory Talons</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you mean Ivory Claws. Maybe Blood Talons?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-15T11:14:15-07:00","id":866019,"title":"","content":"The Ivory Claws tend to 'move among the wealthy and powerful.' So presumably them.<br /> <br /> I don't think the 'other structures' are really rare occasions. Werewolves aren't savages. They start out like everyone else, with the same social inclinations and organizations. Packs are just as likely to be companies as street gangs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-15T11:41:51-07:00","id":866027,"title":"","content":"I did mean Ivory Claws- that was something that seemed to slip through my editing process. Werewolf isn't exactly my strong suit, so I probably did the material a disservice here. Any advice on how to revise this section would be greatly appreciated.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> Some additional content I threw together from Guildhalls of the Deathless and the Secrets of the Covenants spoilers. Enjoy.<br /> <br /> <b>Temple (o - ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite:</b> Safe Place (equal or greater value)<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Cult, or your character specifically, maintains a place of worship exemplifying one aspect of their faith. Actions in support of the Cult\u2019s Purpose or Doctrine gain a bonus equal to the dots of the Merit within the Temple\u2019s walls. For example, a Lancea et Sanctum Temple that espouses resisting the Beast would grant a bonus to resisting Frenzy. Additionally, those blessed by the Temple (IE. Those who participate in a service at the Temple) carry its benefits beyond its walls for a number of days equal to dots in the Merit. During this time, the Cult may use any Rites, Rituals, or other powers against the blessed character as though making physical contact.<br /> <b>Note:</b> Like Safe Place, more than one character may invest dots in this Merit.<br /> <br /> This Merit now serves as a prerequisite to Altar, and, much like Cult itself, will probably have a few Merits branching off of it. This little project is just full of inspiration, and I would like to open it up to the public. I know a lot of people have called for a Cults Bluebook, and some people have even made significant progress along those ends, and I would be extremely grateful if people interested in contributing to this humble bit of homebrew could come forward, turning it into a community project.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 04-15-2016, 06:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-16T14:24:24-07:00","id":866524,"title":"","content":"Hmm. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a>, how well do you think these rules might support Cults As Characters? Such as, instead of Player A playing Character X who has a cult, A plays the cult directly? I'm asking because I'm wondering if just simply integrating the Cult rules here might speed up my development of Institutional Covers (as referenced <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/786694-homebrew-demon-merits?p=847701#post847701\">here</a>).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-16T15:07:24-07:00","id":866537,"title":"","content":"What would a Benefit representing supernatural members of the Cult look like?<br /> <br /> I'm curious about how I would mechanically lay out Vampire members of an Arisen's cult in particular.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-16T15:29:42-07:00","id":866544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=866524#post866524\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a>, how well do you think these rules might support Cults As Characters? Such as, instead of Player A playing Character X who has a cult, A plays the cult directly? I'm asking because I'm wondering if just simply integrating the Cult rules here might speed up my development of Institutional Covers (as referenced <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/786694-homebrew-demon-merits?p=847701#post847701\">here</a>).</div> </div> </div> </div>They support them quite well, honestly. A Cult doesn't go away just because the character who invested the Merit dots dies- the institution still stands, though in the case of a Cult of Personality it takes a pretty serious hit. In the case of Institutional Covers, I'd probably rule that Reach = Power and Grasp = Finesse, with Resistance= Lower of the two, so the Demon could wield the Cult like an extension of themselves. It would be an Eldritch, crawling thing- an organization that builds and controls itself; a miniature God-Machine. In such cases of Cults As Characters, I'd probably still invest in an Agent or Operative, just so that there's this sketch of a character who can be the 'face' of the Cult.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/858523-cults-for-everyone?p=866537#post866537\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What would a Benefit representing supernatural members of the Cult look like?<br /> <br /> I'm curious about how I would mechanically lay out Vampire members of an Arisen's cult in particular.</div> </div> </div> </div>You wouldn't need one, per se, unless you wanted the main body of the Cult to have supernatural members, in which case that's what Lore, Temple, Altar, and the Second Sight Merit chain are for. For just a handful of supernatural members, simply purchase a number of Conspirators/Agents/Operatives and tag them as a specific supernatural creature. The guidelines for that are back on post #34.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 04-16-2016, 06:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T11:45:45-07:00","id":866959,"title":"","content":"Reworked chart for Reach and Grasp, which only goes up to seven now. Thought it was wiser to tone it down a little bit. Advanced Reach or Grasp only goes up to two dots now. Still considering the possibility of Epic Reach or Grasp to give it an extra bump.<br /> <br /> That being said, I'd like to extend my sincere gratitude to everyone who has taken the time to contribute to this thread- Cults for Everyone just ticked past the 1,000 views and 7 likes mark, making this my most well-received solo project ever. Thanks for the support everyone.<br /> <br /> I'm now asking for a bit of help. Any suggestions on anything interesting I could do with Advanced Reach/Grasp and a hypothetical Epic Reach/Grasp beyond just adding dots to the trait and boosting Cult membership?<br /> <br /> ***** <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Reach &amp;/or Grasp</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Favor</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Momentarily inconveniences the organization</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Inconveniences the contact for hours</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Threatens physical harm or significant social backlash</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Threatens the life of the contact or risks ostracizing them</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Threatens the lives of more than one member of the organization</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Requires action by the whole organization, the faction goes to war</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Means certain death for numerous faction members</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 04-17-2016, 06:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-27T13:17:51-07:00","id":901308,"title":"","content":"So, decided to come back and post some new stuff here. I've been working on a Sin-Eater 2E conversion, and the Krewe Building material was too good to pass up for making interesting Merits to tie to a Cult. Also, Mage 2E came out and that had some juicy material to work with as well. Here are some new, and reworked, Merits.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> <b>Hidden Master (ooo, oooo, </b>or <b>ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Reach <i>and</i> Grasp 3<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Regardless of whether your character founded the Cult or simply joined it later, they managed to weasel their way into a position of great power, and then quietly receded into the background. Your character benefits from Status or Mystery Cult Initiation equal to their dots in this Merit. Additionally, you no longer suffer the Drawbacks of the Cult Merit; they have no formal ties to the Cult, and as such don\u2019t suffer any Conditions that apply to members of the Cult.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Without formal ties to the Cult, or standing in a position of visible authority, their means of manipulating the Cult directly are greatly reduced. They may only benefit from a number of dots of Reach and/or Grasp equal to their dots in Hidden Master. They can still gain the benefit of the Cult\u2019s full authority by employing their own Conspirators, or engaging in Social Maneuvering with the Cult\u2019s \u2018leader\u2019.<br /> <br /> <b>Ritual Savvy (oo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Lore 2+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Even if the priests don\u2019t totally understand <i>why</i> they\u2019re gathering the tools and symbols you requested, they at least know what to look for. Roll an appropriate dice pool to gather ingredients or prepare the ritual space. Each success on the roll is considered a bonus die to using Rites, Creative Thaumaturgy, Blood Sorcery, etc. that the supernatural patron of the Cult may \u2018buy\u2019 with a point of Willpower. For maximum effect, gather as many ritualists as you can in advance and have each of them take advantage of this Merit as an Extended Action.<br /> <br /> (Author's Note: These two are reskinned and modified Merits from Mage 2E, Mystery Cult Influence and Ritual Savvy respectively.)<br /> <br /> <b>Potent Rites (o - ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Altar, Temple (equal or greater value)<br /> <b>Effect: </b>When the Cult gathers together, their place of worship truly becomes hallowed ground. What form this ceremony takes is dependent entirely upon the Purpose or Doctrine, but the true key to this effect is sincerity. The participants must be there of their own free will and be open to the idea something meaningful is going on, but they don\u2019t need to be aware that an occult rite is happening. Such a rite takes about three hours to perform, and at the end it produces a temporary sacred place with a dot rating equal to the dots in this Merit. The form this sacred place takes is dependent upon the supernatural being in charge. For an Uratha pack it could be a Locus, for a Mage cabal it could be a Hallow, for an Unchained ring it would count as Subverted Infrastructure, for a Sin-Eater krewe it is a Haunt, and for one of the Deathless it could be treated as a Vestige. The sacred place weakens by a single dot once every (Lore) days.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> You need a certain number of worshippers in order to accrue the metaphysical weight necessary to produce such a sacred space. For a powerful and far-reaching Cult, this isn\u2019t really a problem, but gathering large numbers of worshippers to perform the rite could impair the Cult\u2019s actions for days.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Sacred Place Rating</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Minimum Number of Worshippers</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">50</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">100</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">500</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1000</td> </tr> </table></div> (Author's Note: A little something to replace the Dedicated Altar and Consecrated Altar Merits. Upon review, those didn't make a lot of sense, and this one is more flavourful and comes with a bit more mechanical bite anyways)<br /> <br /> <b>Mythic Aspect (oooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Cult Leader<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Cult Leader taps into the mythic ideal behind their Purpose, Relic, or Doctrine- taking up some of the power of the divinity they use the symbolism of. When taking this Merit, choose an Attribute or a Skill- this cannot be changed after purchase. By spending a point of Willpower (or Supernatural Fuel, depending on Template) they may increase that Skill or Attribute by +3 for the scene, even above their normal Skill/Attribute limitations. This Merit may only be taken once.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>By tapping into the symbolic power behind the Cult, the leader starts to accept the ethical or even spiritual authority of the Cult. In any scene where they take advantage of this Merit, they suffer a Breaking Point with a -2 penalty should they break one of the laws outlined in the Cult\u2019s doctrine.<br /> <br /> <b>Mythic Avatar (ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Mythic Aspect, Supernatural Resistance 3+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Cult\u2019s leader acquires the power to become the physical embodiment of the Cult\u2019s god, becoming a half-divine being from one perspective, or a twisted monstrosity from another. By spending a point of Willpower and a point of Supernatrual Fuel (or two Willpower if they do not possess a Supernatural Fuel trait) spiritual power explodes from the character, surrounding and merging with them, transforming them into a being with the following characteristics. The transformation lasts for a number of turns equal to Supernatural Resistance + Composure.<ul><li>+2 to Stamina and Presence, and +4 to one Attribute that is suitably related with the Aspect\u2019s nature. This can take them above their normal limitations.</li> <li>The character\u2019s unarmed attacks may inflict Lethal damage- the means by which they accomplish this vary depending on the nature and symbolism of the Aspect. If they already possess a means by which to inflict Lethal damage unarmed, then this adds a +1 weapon modifier.</li> <li>The character gains a number of points of Armor equal to their Supernatural Resistance which they may divide as they see fit.</li> <li>The character may interact with anything in Twilight.</li> </ul><b>Drawback: </b>Accepting the transformation means giving in to a kind of divine power, enabling the character to act without compunction. The exact means by which this occurs varies Cult to Cult, it could be a temporary Claiming by a Spirit, a manifestation of a Beast\u2019s Horror, an expression of a Vampire\u2019s Beast, or something else. Regardless, the power held strains the soul. At the end of the scene the character suffers a Breaking Point.<br /> <br /> <b>Glorious Divinity (ooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Mythic Avatar<br /> <b>Effect: </b>When the character makes use of Mythic Avatar, they gain access to a Dread Power they wouldn\u2019t be able to otherwise use.<br /> <b>Drawback:</b> For every purchase of this Merit, the Breaking Point following the use of Mythic Avatar is made with a -1 penalty.<br /> <br /> <b>Sending Forth the Aspect (oo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Mythic Aspect<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The bonds of initiation and belief unite Cultist and Leader, enabling the Cult Leader to be present in spirit even when they cannot be present in the flesh. The target must be a member of the Cult and within the Leader\u2019s line of sight, or under the effect of worshipping at a Temple. The leader spends a Willpower point and rolls Resolve + Composure (+ Supernatural Resistance), the target can choose to resist with a roll of their own, but should the Leader succeed they send forth their Aspect which treats the target as a Fetter, except that it can use its powers on its surroundings The Aspect functions like a Dream Form, resembles an image of the deity, or something formed from Mythic Avatar, and may use any abilities the Leader can. It gains the Twilight Form and Materialize Manifestations for free. The Aspect is always visible to the target, and may be seen with suitable Unseen Senses. The target and Leader may communicate through the Aspect, verbally or telepathically, but this does not include subconscious or hidden information. The Aspect remains for a number of hours equal to successes on the activation roll, and the Leader may reset the time limit by spending another Willpower point.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>The Leader must concentrate in order to use this ability. If their physical body is injured, engages in combat, uses a supernatural power on their end, or otherwise suffers a serious distraction, the use of this Merit ends.<br /> <br /> <b>Ascension (ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Mythic Avatar, Supernatural Resistance 6+, Advanced Reach or Grasp 2<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The faith of such a massive Cult is sustaining to a Cult Leader who has invested in taking up the mantle of a god can truly become one; the character gains all the benefits of the Immortal Dread Power and stops aging. This functions differently for Deathless- they may grant themselves a Descent Reset by suppressing a Willpower <i>dot</i>, but suffer a Breaking Point with a penalty equal to the number of resets they granted themselves once they <i>do</i> Descend. Suppressed Willpower dots return when they lose a dot of Sekhem.<br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Though Immortal, they gain a Bane. Additionally, should the primary Cult (and not any Splinter Organizations, Operatives, etc.) lose its Advanced Reach or Grasp rating, the benefits of this Merit are suppressed and they begin to age normally again.<br /> <br /> (Author's Note: The following are all edited from Geist, except for Glorious Divinity, which is something of my own creation. These Merits explicitly stack with supernatural powers and natural capabilities, so enjoy making a seriously hulked-out character with Mythic Aspect and Mythic Avatar. The Mummy addition to the Ascension Merit was inspired by the Affinity &quot;Speaking with the Master's Voice&quot;.)<br /> <br /> <b>Symbolic Potency (ooo ; ooooo)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Mystery Cult Initiation (Equal or Greater value)<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The symbolism and teachings of the Cult have truly ingrained themselves on this particular character, making their use of supernatural abilities significantly more potent. At three dots, when incorporating suitable symbolism they gain the 8-again quality to the activation roll and regain a point of Supernatural Fuel on a success. At five dots, they gain the rote action quality to the activation roll and regain a point of Willpower and a point of Supernatural Fuel.<br /> <br /> (Author's Note: A little something to represent a dedicated mystical Cultist. A Mage with this Merit can really make the most of Order Yantras.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-08T08:07:20-07:00","id":910089,"title":"","content":"So, transplanting this from <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/845732-pacts-for-everyone?p=909192#post909192\">here</a>.<br /> <br /> <u><b>The Honorable Order of Soon To Be Renamed Hedge Sorcerers</b></u><br /> <b>Concept</b>: Some time ago, the founder of the organization made a <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/845732-pacts-for-everyone\">Pact</a> for power, both for themselves and for those who would follow them on their mission. All members of the organization swear into in and become signatories into this initial Pact, which grants them certain powers (Contracts) and responsibilities. Additional, specialized, initiations are encouraged, as is bargaining for additional power from approved sources (subject to internal review). Together they use Hedge Sorcery (Contracts) to accomplish the organization's goals.<br /> <br /> <b>Needs</b>: More established founder, a concrete Mission or Creed, the Pact spelled out, Cult structure, an actual name.<br /> <br /> [<u>Hedge Sorcery (and Hedge Sorcerers)</u>: Mortals who beg, bargain, or steal access to Contracts (as seen in Changeling the Lost 2E). Lacking Glamour (usually) they activate them through their Catches. When Wyrd is required in an effect (but not the roll), treat them as if they had Wyrd 1, unless otherwise noted.]","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-26T11:13:51-07:00","id":937695,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a> , do you mind if I use these cult rules as part of my fansplat project, Demigod, the Empyrean? It's just that they seem to fit extremely well and it seems a little silly to come up with a whole new system for cults when there's a fully fleshed-out one already there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}